window.CAVE_CASES = [{"id": "S01", "title": "Cabin inertia", "domain": "Dynamics", "question_id": "scene_01/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nApparatus and coordinates. A fixed camera near the rear of a closed cabin looks toward its front. Vehicle-local+z is forward, away from the camera; +x is the vehicle's right. Image-left/right roughly follow vehicle-left/right, but perspective and object height affect projection. An upward image displacement is not by itself a forward displacement. Fixed cabin edges, table, bowl, roof supports and tether base are spatial references. The blue ball and its shallow bowl are on the central longitudinal line of the table, not on its right side. A red pendulum bob, when present, hangs from a roof support toward the far-left; a green helium balloon, when present, is tethered to a fixed anchor toward the far-right. There is NO yellow block, slider tray, linear guide or passive slider spring in any sample. A sample contains all three response bodies, or omits one complete red-bob, green-balloon or blue-ball body/force assembly before the experiment starts. An empty bowl may remain when the blue ball is absent. Establish which bodies actually exist in F0, track those same bodies and their fixed supports, and never infer a control from an absent object. Body existence is fixed throughout one sample and is not itself an event. Small supported module positions and initial positions can vary between experiments.\n\nPhysics. Gravity is downward. The vehicle moves forward throughout and never reverses or resets between events. The dense bob and ball respond through inertia, gravity, their suspension or bowl constraints, friction and real contacts. The lighter-than-air balloon responds to cabin-air acceleration, the associated pressure gradient, drag and its tether; its transient need not follow a dense body. Air/buoyancy is a reduced-order physical model, not full CFD. Steering and speed changes can act simultaneously. Pendulum oscillation, rolling, balloon motion and real contacts persist into later events. No object is repositioned at an event boundary. The ball's curved support and the pendulum/tether constraints turn different components of effective acceleration into different movements, so compare fore-aft and lateral changes relative to supports, combine the remaining response bodies and account for inherited motion. A coloured body is not a direction lamp or a painted operation label. In a single still, neither one body's largest image displacement nor its apparent height uniquely specifies both controls.\n\nHidden controls. The only allowed labels are Accelerate, Brake, TurnLeft and TurnRight. Accelerate increases forward speed; Brake reduces it without reversing. TurnLeft and TurnRight steer relative to the vehicle's own forward direction. In a top view with forward upward, left steering is counterclockwise and right steering clockwise. An event applies one primitive or two simultaneous primitives. A pair contains at most one longitudinal control, Accelerate or Brake, and at most one steering control, TurnLeft or TurnRight. Duplicate or opposite controls on the same axis never occur together. For each event report the unordered set, including both labels if two controls occurred. No fixed ordering or repetition pattern is promised.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach evidence image is a true single-camera frame from the corresponding active response window, starting 0.30 s after onset and ending before ramp-down. A fixed geometric visibility rule chooses a frame with available bodies moving, balancing fore-aft and lateral clues. The images are not deliberately delayed settled snapshots; capture times are not uniformly spaced. Constraints, inherited motion, rotation, perspective and absent bodies still require interpretation.\nCapture times (physical simulation clock; the F0 image precedes event 1): F1=3.133347 s; F2=7.399973 s; F3=12.266565 s; F4=18.233141 s; F5=21.333094 s; F6=27.366335 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nAccelerate, Brake, TurnLeft, TurnRight\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"Accelerate\"],[\"Brake\"],[\"TurnLeft\"],[\"TurnRight\"],[\"Accelerate\",\"TurnLeft\"],[\"Accelerate\",\"TurnRight\"],[\"Brake\",\"TurnLeft\"],[\"Brake\",\"TurnRight\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"Accelerate\"], [\"Brake\"], [\"TurnLeft\"], [\"TurnRight\"], [\"Accelerate\", \"TurnLeft\"], [\"Accelerate\", \"TurnRight\"], [\"Brake\", \"TurnLeft\"], [\"Brake\", \"TurnRight\"]].", "excerpt": "The bob is far left (356, 236), the ball is at 507, 368, and the balloon is displaced right and large. Bob left with the balloon right suggests lateral acceleration to the left of the image, so TurnRight. The ball is higher, which suggests Brake. Best guess: Brake + TurnRight.", "excerpt_event": 6, "response_text": "This is a best-supported guess from rough image cues, not a rigorous derivation.\n\nAssumed response rules:\n- Accelerate: the bob swings backward (toward the camera, appearing lower in the image), the ball rolls backward, and the balloon leans forward.\n- Brake: the opposite of Accelerate.\n- TurnLeft: the vehicle accelerates leftward, so dense bodies move right in the image relative to the cabin; for a right turn they move left. The balloon moves opposite to the dense bodies.\n\nFrame-by-frame readings (F0 baseline: bob at x≈395, y≈237; ball at 477, 383; balloon upright):\n- **F1:** The bob moves right and lower (435, 231) and the ball moves right and lower (508, 384). The balloon is unchanged. Rightward dense motion suggests TurnLeft. The bob's lower position suggests a mild Accelerate, but the bob's height is ambiguous. Best guess: Accelerate + TurnLeft.\n- **F2:** The bob is at 398, 240 and the ball has moved left and up (452, 372). The bob is near center and the ball is left and forward. Forward ball motion suggests Brake. Left ball motion suggests TurnRight. Best guess: Brake + TurnRight.\n- **F3:** The bob is far left (380, 234), the ball is right and lower (510, 384), and the balloon leans slightly left. Bob left and ball right is inconsistent, probably from inherited swing. Ball right suggests TurnLeft, and its lower position suggests Accelerate. Best guess: Accelerate + TurnLeft.\n- **F4:** The bob is at 402, 209, higher in the image, which suggests the bob moved forward. The ball is at 504, 366, up and right. The balloon leans left and back, so the air accelerates right. Best guess: Brake + TurnLeft.\n- **F5:** The bob is at 381, 232. The ball is at 442, 383, left and lower. The balloon is slightly right. Ball left suggests TurnRight, and lower suggests Accelerate. Best guess: Accelerate + TurnRight.\n- **F6:** The bob is far left (356, 236), the ball is at 507, 368, and the balloon is displaced right and large. Bob left with the balloon right suggests lateral acceleration to the left of the image, so TurnRight. The ball is higher, which suggests Brake. Best guess: Brake + TurnRight.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["Accelerate", "TurnLeft"], "predicted": ["Accelerate", "TurnLeft"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["Brake", "TurnRight"], "predicted": ["Brake", "TurnRight"], "correct": true}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["Accelerate", "TurnLeft"], "predicted": ["Accelerate", "TurnLeft"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["Brake", "TurnLeft"], "predicted": ["Brake", "TurnLeft"], "correct": true}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["Accelerate", "TurnRight"], "predicted": ["Accelerate", "TurnRight"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["Brake", "TurnLeft"], "predicted": ["Brake", "TurnRight"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S02", "title": "Rotation & gas", "domain": "Dynamics", "question_id": "scene_06/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nApparatus and view: a circular deck rotates about its vertical central shaft inside an opaque cabin. The interior camera is fixed above the deck, looking downward; it does not rotate with the deck. Two or three radial assemblies lie inside the rim. Each contains a solid coloured load on a sliding carriage, a radial guide, and a spring connecting the carriage to an inner anchor. Red, blue and amber load assemblies have masses of 0.65, 0.80 and 1.00 kg and spring stiffnesses of 1.0625, 1.53 and 2.21 N/m respectively. A small mechanical pointer near the central spindle is mounted on the stationary shaft, with a torsion spring and viscous coupling to the deck.\n\nA transparent, sealed, water-filled semicircular channel is fixed near the outer edge of the deck. Its ends are closed. It contains one small red, gas-filled thin-shell sphere, approximately 96 mm in diameter and 4 g in combined shell-and-gas mass. The sphere is separate from the larger loads on the radial carriages. The outer motor and its four-detent controller are outside the supplied interior view. The deck starts at rest. No positions, velocities, spring states or liquid-object states are reset between events.\n\nControls: clockwise and counterclockwise are defined as viewed from above. ClockwiseAccelerate sets the motor to the clockwise high-speed detent, nominally 30 degrees/s. ClockwiseDecelerate changes clockwise rotation to the low-speed detent, nominally 12 degrees/s. CounterclockwiseAccelerate sets the counterclockwise high-speed detent, nominally 30 degrees/s. CounterclockwiseDecelerate changes counterclockwise rotation to the low-speed detent, nominally 12 degrees/s. Selecting a high-speed detent may reverse the previous direction. Each event selects one complete motor setting.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe evidence frame for each event is selected from its 2.2-4.0 s response window. Motor commands are 5.0 s apart. The deck starts at rest and all mechanical and liquid-object states subsequently continue as described above.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=1.966667 s; F1=6.000000 s; F2=11.000000 s; F3=16.000000 s; F4=21.000000 s; F5=26.000000 s; F6=31.000000 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2 s, 7 s, 12 s, 17 s, 22 s, 27 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nClockwiseAccelerate, ClockwiseDecelerate, CounterclockwiseAccelerate, CounterclockwiseDecelerate\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"ClockwiseAccelerate\"],[\"ClockwiseDecelerate\"],[\"CounterclockwiseAccelerate\"],[\"CounterclockwiseDecelerate\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"ClockwiseAccelerate\"], [\"ClockwiseDecelerate\"], [\"CounterclockwiseAccelerate\"], [\"CounterclockwiseDecelerate\"]].", "excerpt": "F1 (t=6): red at about 4 o'clock (~ -20°). The deck turned clockwise by about 110°. Event 1 began at 2 s, so 4 s elapsed. Ramping toward 30°/s would give roughly 100°, so event 1 is ClockwiseAccelerate.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "Estimating deck angle from the red assembly position (viewed from above):\n- F0: red at 12 o'clock (angle 90°). Capture at 1.97 s, before any event, so this is the reference.\n- F1 (t=6): red at about 4 o'clock (~ -20°). The deck turned clockwise by about 110°. Event 1 began at 2 s, so 4 s elapsed. Ramping toward 30°/s would give roughly 100°, so event 1 is ClockwiseAccelerate.\n- F2 (t=11): red at about 6 o'clock (-100°), a further 80° clockwise over 5 s, about 16°/s. The speed fell to roughly the 12°/s detent, so event 2 is ClockwiseDecelerate.\n- F3 (t=16): red at about 11 o'clock (~125°). Going from -100° to 125° clockwise is -100 → -235, i.e. 135° clockwise, about 27°/s. That fits ClockwiseAccelerate, so event 3 is ClockwiseAccelerate.\n- F4 (t=21): red at about 9 o'clock (~180°). From 125° clockwise would go to about 125 - 60 = 65°, but the observed 180° is 55° counterclockwise, which could also be 305° clockwise. The channel position also shifted: the channel in F3 spans the right side, and in F4 it spans the top-left. Using the red assembly (F3 at about 125°, F4 at about 180°), a counterclockwise move of 55° over 5 s is about 11°/s, matching the low detent in the counterclockwise direction. Event 4 would be CounterclockwiseDecelerate, but that would require counterclockwise motion already in progress, so it must be CounterclockwiseAccelerate with the transient still ramping. The measured 55° is less than the 150° expected at 30°/s, so the ramp-up is slow. At 12°/s, 60° would fit well. I'll choose CounterclockwiseAccelerate since a reversal needs a high-speed detent.\n- F5 (t=26): red at about 6 o'clock (-100°=260°). From 180° that is 80° counterclockwise, about 16°/s, which is a lower speed. Event 5 is CounterclockwiseDecelerate.\n- F6 (t=31): red at about 9 o'clock (180°). From 260° to 180° is 80° clockwise, so the direction reversed to clockwise. Event 6 is ClockwiseAccelerate.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["ClockwiseAccelerate"], "predicted": ["ClockwiseAccelerate"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["ClockwiseDecelerate"], "predicted": ["ClockwiseDecelerate"], "correct": true}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["ClockwiseAccelerate"], "predicted": ["ClockwiseAccelerate"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["CounterclockwiseAccelerate"], "predicted": ["CounterclockwiseAccelerate"], "correct": true}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["CounterclockwiseDecelerate"], "predicted": ["CounterclockwiseDecelerate"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["ClockwiseAccelerate"], "predicted": ["ClockwiseAccelerate"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": true}, {"id": "S03", "title": "Driven pendulums", "domain": "Dynamics", "question_id": "scene_11/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nA carriage moves along a horizontal straight rail. Three rigid pendulums hang from its overhead beam: the burgundy pendulum has a suspension length of 0.72 m, the teal pendulum 1.08 m, and the copper-coloured pendulum 1.52 m. Their suspension slots may be permuted between experiments but remain fixed within one experiment. Some experiments also contain a smaller ivory-coloured passive pendulum. The camera is fixed to the carriage; image-right corresponds to its horizontal +x direction. The walls, floor and beam belong to the carriage. Gravitational acceleration is 9.81 m/s^2, and the pendulum pivots have passive damping.\n\nFor each event, an external controller selects one fixed profile A, B or C. After the selector has moved, the carriage translates 4.40 m using that profile. In the following expression, t is time since the translation starts, x0 is its starting position, and s is +1 or -1 for rightward or leftward travel. Travel directions alternate, starting with rightward travel in the first event. Travel direction is not the label to predict.\n\nDefine Q(u) = 0 for u <= 0, Q(u) = 10u^3 - 15u^4 + 6u^5 for 0 < u < 1, and Q(u) = 1 for u >= 1.\nAll three profiles have the form\nx_j(t) = x0 + s * 4.40 * [Q(t/2.50) + w_j * Q((t-d_j)/2.50)] / (1+w_j), in metres.\n\nA: d_A = 0.865523 s, w_A = 0.833172; label TrajectoryA.\nB: d_B = 1.052630 s, w_B = 0.814545; label TrajectoryB.\nC: d_C = 1.245535 s, w_C = 0.793962; label TrajectoryC.\nEach profile remains at its endpoint after t = 2.50 + d_j seconds. The controller makes no additional changes to the profile in response to the pendulums' instantaneous state. The carriage and pendulums are not reset between events.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach evidence image is taken after the selected carriage translation has stopped and before the next selection. Selections are 6.0 s apart; translation begins 0.80 s after selection. Use the supplied physical-clock capture times with the public profile equations. Pendulum oscillations persist between events.\nCapture times (physical simulation clock declared in the public apparatus protocol): F0=2.000000 s; F1=7.200000 s; F2=13.200000 s; F3=19.200000 s; F4=25.200000 s; F5=31.200000 s; F6=37.200000 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2 s, 8 s, 14 s, 20 s, 26 s, 32 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nTrajectoryA, TrajectoryB, TrajectoryC\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"TrajectoryA\"],[\"TrajectoryB\"],[\"TrajectoryC\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"TrajectoryA\"], [\"TrajectoryB\"], [\"TrajectoryC\"]].", "excerpt": "In the frames, the pendulum in the middle slot (the burgundy-looking one, apparently the long copper pendulum) swings the most in F4 and F6, and the left one swings less. That would point to the long pendulum being left unsuppressed in those events, which argues against C there. The evidence is too weak to resolve further, so the answer below is a guess.", "excerpt_event": 6, "response_text": "I could not derive these labels by simulation or measurement. They are a best guess from a rough reading of the frames, with no reliable evidence behind any individual event.\n\nThe physics suggests residual oscillation after each move depends on the profile's delay and weight relative to each pendulum's period. The three periods are about 1.69 s (0.72 m), 2.08 s (1.08 m) and 2.48 s (1.52 m). A double-pulse profile cancels a mode when its delay is about half that mode's period. The delays are 0.866 s, 1.053 s and 1.246 s, which are roughly half of 1.73 s, 2.1 s and 2.49 s. So A suppresses the short pendulum, B the medium one, and C the long one.\n\nIn the frames, the pendulum in the middle slot (the burgundy-looking one, apparently the long copper pendulum) swings the most in F4 and F6, and the left one swings less. That would point to the long pendulum being left unsuppressed in those events, which argues against C there. The evidence is too weak to resolve further, so the answer below is a guess.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["TrajectoryB"], "predicted": ["TrajectoryB"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["TrajectoryC"], "predicted": ["TrajectoryA"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["TrajectoryB"], "predicted": ["TrajectoryB"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["TrajectoryA"], "predicted": ["TrajectoryA"], "correct": true}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["TrajectoryB"], "predicted": ["TrajectoryB"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["TrajectoryA"], "predicted": ["TrajectoryA"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S04", "title": "Tray impact", "domain": "Dynamics", "question_id": "scene_12/sample_0133/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0133", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nThe rigid rectangular tray supports eight free physical response bodies: a tall burgundy tower toward the upper-left, a teal sphere toward the upper-middle/right, an ivory body toward the upper-right that can be a sphere, box, or cylinder, an ochre sliding drum toward the lower-left, a low brass puck toward the lower-middle, a short teal block right of the middle, a rolling ivory barrel toward the lower-right, and a ruby-colored ball toward the upper-middle/left. Those positions are approximate and can vary; establish the actual identities and positions from F0 and track them through the sequence. Fixed tray lines and the rim are spatial references, not control indicators. Small colored pieces near the upper-left or upper-right rim are stationary, non-colliding layout references rather than response bodies; their count and arrangement can vary across samples but do not change with the hidden controls. The camera is rigidly carried by the struck tray, looks straight down, and preserves its tray-relative pose. Image-right is tray-local +x, image-up is tray-local +z. Screen-left/right/up/down are not world gravitational up/down.\n\nFour exterior hammers can strike four different sides of the tray. IMPORTANT: operation names indicate the struck side, not the direction of the inward force. LeftImpact means the west/left hammer strikes inward toward +x; RightImpact means the east/right hammer strikes inward toward -x. UpImpact means the far/top (+z) hammer strikes inward toward -z; DownImpact means the near/bottom (-z) hammer strikes inward toward +z. An event contains either one primitive or two different perpendicular primitives applied simultaneously. The first event always uses two perpendicular primitives so the tower can fall clear of the adjacent wall; each later event may use one or two. This is a common protocol constraint, not a cue to either first-event direction. Opposite pairs and repeated identical primitives within one event are not permitted. The permitted primitive labels are LeftImpact, RightImpact, UpImpact, and DownImpact.\n\nThe hammers physically collide with the dynamically supported tray; they do not directly push the free response bodies. The tray recoils, and its carried camera observes how the bodies lag relative to the accelerating tray through inertia, contact friction, rolling, sliding, and possible tipping. Two hammers can jointly produce two recoil components. The tray's passive return springs and dampers also affect the later trajectory. A sphere can retain rolling velocity and a toppled tower remains toppled; no body is reset between events. Do not identify later events from the already fallen tower alone. Compare new tray-relative changes in several separated low bodies with rolling-body changes, while accounting for history and contacts. Neither one instantaneous position nor a world-frame displacement alone determines the hidden controls.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe experiment has an initial 3.0 s hold, then 5.5 s event intervals. Hammer contact occurs about 2.067 s into an interval. Images show the evolving tray-carried view, not contact instants or guaranteed equilibrium. Existing rolling velocity, contacts and a fallen tower persist.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=2.966667 s; F1=7.333333 s; F2=11.500000 s; F3=17.000000 s; F4=22.500000 s; F5=28.000000 s; F6=33.500000 s.\nSupplied event start times: 3 s, 8.5 s, 14 s, 19.5 s, 25 s, 30.5 s. The first event's evidence is sampled about 4.333 s into its interval; the other supplied event images are about 3.000 s into their intervals.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nLeftImpact, RightImpact, UpImpact, DownImpact\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"LeftImpact\"],[\"RightImpact\"],[\"UpImpact\"],[\"DownImpact\"],[\"LeftImpact\",\"UpImpact\"],[\"DownImpact\",\"LeftImpact\"],[\"RightImpact\",\"UpImpact\"],[\"DownImpact\",\"RightImpact\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"LeftImpact\"], [\"RightImpact\"], [\"UpImpact\"], [\"DownImpact\"], [\"LeftImpact\", \"UpImpact\"], [\"DownImpact\", \"LeftImpact\"], [\"RightImpact\", \"UpImpact\"], [\"DownImpact\", \"RightImpact\"]].", "excerpt": "the ivory body moved from (601,186) to (575,186), the block from (537,238) to (511,238), the drum from (321,348) to (295,348), and the puck from (399,368) to (373,368). This is a left shift only, so the tray moved right: LeftImpact.", "excerpt_event": 3, "response_text": "Reading the frames as tray-relative displacements, with image-up as +z:\n\n- **F0 to F1:** the tower fell toward the upper-left. Every body shifted left and up: the ivory body moved from (602,187) to (575,160), the teal block from (538,238) to (511,211), the ochre drum from (322,348) to (295,321), and the barrel from (642,348) to (617,330).\n- A body lags the tray, so a uniform shift of about (-27, -27) px means the tray accelerated toward the right and toward the bottom.\n- Right-and-down tray motion means the tray was pushed toward +x and -z. LeftImpact pushes toward +x, and UpImpact pushes toward -z. Event 1 is therefore Left+Up.\n- **F1 to F2:** everything shifted right and down by about (+26, +27). The tray accelerated left and up, which means RightImpact (toward -x) plus DownImpact (toward +z). Event 2 is Right+Down.\n- **F2 to F3:** the ivory body moved from (601,186) to (575,186), the block from (537,238) to (511,238), the drum from (321,348) to (295,348), and the puck from (399,368) to (373,368). This is a left shift only, so the tray moved right: LeftImpact.\n- **F3 to F4:** the ivory body moved from (575,186) to (602,160), the block from (511,238) to (535,211), the drum from (295,348) to (318,324), and the barrel from (614,355) to (641,330). The shift is right and up, so the tray moved left and down. Left motion is RightImpact, and motion toward -z is UpImpact. Event 4 is Right+Up.\n- **F4 to F5:** the ivory body moved from (602,160) to (628,160), the block from (535,211) to (560,211), and the drum from (318,324) to (343,324). This is a right shift only, so the tray moved left: RightImpact.\n- **F5 to F6:** the ivory body moved from (628,160) to (601,186), the block from (560,211) to (533,238), and the drum from (343,324) to (315,352). The shift is left and down, so the tray moved right and up. Right motion is LeftImpact, and motion toward +z is DownImpact. Event 6 is Down+Left.", "frames": ["worked/F0.png", "worked/F1.png", "worked/F2.png", "worked/F3.png", "worked/F4.png", "worked/F5.png", "worked/F6.png"], "video": "worked/dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["LeftImpact", "UpImpact"], "predicted": ["LeftImpact", "UpImpact"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["DownImpact", "RightImpact"], "predicted": ["RightImpact", "DownImpact"], "correct": true}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["LeftImpact"], "predicted": ["LeftImpact"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["RightImpact", "UpImpact"], "predicted": ["RightImpact", "UpImpact"], "correct": true}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["RightImpact"], "predicted": ["RightImpact"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["DownImpact", "LeftImpact"], "predicted": ["DownImpact", "LeftImpact"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": true}, {"id": "S05", "title": "Moving heat source", "domain": "Thermal", "question_id": "scene_03/sample_0025/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0025", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS\nA fixed camera looks down from the near edge of a rectangular conductive plate. Plate x points toward image-right; plate z points toward the far/top edge. One heater beneath the plate moves on a two-axis gantry. Three other heaters are stationary beneath the plate, approximately at the far-left, far-right and near-right. Their installation positions and the moving heater's initial position vary between experiments. All four heaters have the same constant power and heating footprint. The interior images show a temperature-sensitive coating and warm-air haze above the plate, but not the underside heaters or gantry.\n\nThe coating has three horizontal bands separated by two narrow grey installation seams: a broad near band, a narrow middle band, and a broad far band. All bands use the same temperature-to-colour scale. Their nominal heating-response times are 0.35, 1.80 and 4.20 seconds respectively; their cooling-response times are 1.55 times those values. These are properties of the installed coating, not additional controls. The experiment runs continuously without resetting positions, temperature or coating state between events.\n\nCONTROLS\nMoveLeft moves the mobile heater toward decreasing plate x; MoveRight moves it toward increasing plate x. MoveUp moves it toward increasing plate z, toward the far edge; MoveDown moves it toward decreasing plate z, toward the near edge. Each active gantry axis has a nominal motor speed of 0.30 metres per second during the 2.6-second command, with smooth motor start and stop. An event can be one control or two controls applied simultaneously. Infer the complete set of controls for each event.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event moves the heater for 2.6 s and is followed by 2.9 s of observation. Its evidence image is a native interior frame after that movement has finished and before the next movement begins. Residual heat and coating response remain visible.\nCapture times (physical simulation clock; the F0 image precedes event 1): F1=7.433306 s; F2=12.799891 s; F3=18.433138 s; F4=23.933054 s; F5=29.432970 s; F6=34.833534 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2 s, 7.5 s, 13 s, 18.5 s, 24 s, 29.5 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nMoveLeft, MoveRight, MoveUp, MoveDown\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"MoveLeft\"],[\"MoveRight\"],[\"MoveUp\"],[\"MoveDown\"],[\"MoveLeft\",\"MoveUp\"],[\"MoveDown\",\"MoveLeft\"],[\"MoveRight\",\"MoveUp\"],[\"MoveDown\",\"MoveRight\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"MoveLeft\"], [\"MoveRight\"], [\"MoveUp\"], [\"MoveDown\"], [\"MoveLeft\", \"MoveUp\"], [\"MoveDown\", \"MoveLeft\"], [\"MoveRight\", \"MoveUp\"], [\"MoveDown\", \"MoveRight\"]].", "excerpt": "the spot is at about x=500, y=290 in F0 and about x=440, y=295 in F1. It shifted left by about 60 px, with little vertical change. Event 1 is MoveLeft.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I tracked the mobile heater's hot spot across frames. The three stationary heaters sit at far-left, far-right and near-right. The mobile heater is the one that moves, and it starts near the middle of the plate, around image x≈500, at the seam between the far and middle bands.\n\nApproximate hot-spot positions per frame, with the sign of each shift:\n\n- **F0 to F1:** the spot is at about x=500, y=290 in F0 and about x=440, y=295 in F1. It shifted left by about 60 px, with little vertical change. Event 1 is MoveLeft.\n- **F1 to F2:** the spot is at about x=420, y=295 in F2. The lower blob is at y≈295, spanning x 300-520, so it moved only slightly left. Event 2 is probably MoveLeft. Because the mobile heater can only move 0.78 m per command, the small change might instead mean a mix of left and down, but the evidence is weak.\n- **F2 to F3:** the blob is at about x=340, y=290 and appears slightly further left. In F3 the lower blob is at about x=340, y=300 and merges with the far-left heater. Event 3 is MoveLeft.\n- **F3 to F4:** the blob merges at about x=330, y=280, and the far-left merge extends to x=480 at y≈270. This suggests a move up and slightly right. A pure MoveUp would raise the spot into the far band, which responds slowly, so the visible change is small. I choose MoveUp.\n- **F4 to F5:** a new hot spot appears at about x=500, y=295, to the right. Event 5 is MoveRight.\n- **F5 to F6:** the spot now sits at about x=420, y=350, lower and slightly left of before. Event 6 is MoveDown with MoveLeft.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["MoveLeft"], "predicted": ["MoveLeft"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["MoveLeft", "MoveUp"], "predicted": ["MoveLeft"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["MoveDown"], "predicted": ["MoveLeft"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["MoveRight", "MoveUp"], "predicted": ["MoveUp"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["MoveDown", "MoveRight"], "predicted": ["MoveRight"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["MoveDown", "MoveLeft"], "predicted": ["MoveDown", "MoveLeft"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S06", "title": "Layered liquids", "domain": "Thermal", "question_id": "scene_08/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nApparatus: a clear upright cylindrical vessel contains two liquid layers: air-free water below and Shin-Etsu KF-96L-2cs dimethyl silicone fluid above. Both are initially at 2.0 degrees Celsius. Each layer is connected to a separate external temperature-control jacket. The jackets, source valves and layer selector are outside the supplied interior view. The camera looks slightly down from the front at the vessel, its lower rim, the liquid interface, the upper free surface and fifteen freely moving sealed floats. Image-up is approximately vertical up. The vessel and camera remain fixed during a sample.\n\nEach float has a transparent spherical envelope, a coloured core and a small metal ballast. There are three floats of each core family. Their calibrated effective densities are: amber flattened core, 999.93 kg/m3; copper-red capsule, 999.95 kg/m3; green cylinder, 896.00 kg/m3; pale-blue rounded core, 896.20 kg/m3; violet capsule, 950.00 kg/m3. Colours identify fixed float families and do not change with temperature. This simulation treats the floats as rigid, constant-volume objects and the two liquids as well-mixed layers. It models bulk buoyancy, drag and solid contact, but does not resolve interfacial capillary forces or convection. Float positions, velocities and both liquid temperatures are not reset between controls.\n\nThere are four complete control choices. UpperLayer+Heat connects the upper layer's jacket to the 3.8 degrees Celsius source. UpperLayer+Cool connects that jacket to the 0.2 degrees Celsius source. LowerLayer+Heat and LowerLayer+Cool make the corresponding source connections for the lower layer. Each event selects exactly one of these four layer-and-source combinations; the mechanical valves take finite time to change over.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe initial image is captured immediately before the first valve command. Each event image is a single original frame five seconds after the corresponding command begins. Commands are 6.0 s apart, and liquid temperatures and float motion continue evolving.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=2.000000 s; F1=7.000000 s; F2=13.000000 s; F3=19.000000 s; F4=25.000000 s; F5=31.000000 s; F6=37.000000 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2 s, 8 s, 14 s, 20 s, 26 s, 32 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nLowerLayer, UpperLayer, Heat, Cool\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"Heat\",\"LowerLayer\"],[\"Cool\",\"LowerLayer\"],[\"Heat\",\"UpperLayer\"],[\"Cool\",\"UpperLayer\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"Heat\", \"LowerLayer\"], [\"Cool\", \"LowerLayer\"], [\"Heat\", \"UpperLayer\"], [\"Cool\", \"UpperLayer\"]].", "excerpt": "F0 to F1: the red and amber floats sank slightly. This means the lower layer was cooled (event 1 = Cool Lower), and the upper layer is unchanged.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "Physical reasoning: water's density peaks near 4 °C. Water floats (amber and copper-red are about 999.93 and 999.95 kg/m3) are nearly neutral at 2 °C. Warming the lower water toward 3.8 °C makes it denser, so the floats rise. Cooling it toward 0.2 °C makes it less dense, so the floats sink.\n\nF0 to F1: the red and amber floats sank slightly. This means the lower layer was cooled (event 1 = Cool Lower), and the upper layer is unchanged.\n\nF1 to F2: the floats sank further to the bottom, so the lower layer was cooled again (event 2 = Cool Lower). The upper floats (green, blue) rose to the top. The upper silicone fluid has a density near 896 and the floats are at 896.0 and 896.2. Silicone expands strongly when heated, so heating makes the fluid less dense and the floats sink. Cooling makes the fluid denser and the floats rise. The floats rose, so the upper layer was cooled. The violet floats moved from the interface up into the upper layer, which fits an upper cooling. The F2 image is at 13 s, and the event 2 command began at 8 s, so the upper cooling was probably event 2 (the F1 image at 7 s shows no upper change yet). Event 1 started at 2 s, and F1 at 7 s shows the lower floats sinking, so event 1 = Cool Lower. Event 2 produced the upper rise and the lower sinking continued, but only one control applies per event. If event 2 = Cool Upper, the lower layer is still cooling from its earlier temperature, so its floats keep sinking. That fits.\n\nF2 to F3: the upper floats are unchanged. The lower floats sank further, though that may be only continued sinking. Event 3 is ambiguous: it could be Cool Lower or Cool Upper. Since the lower floats continue downward in F3 and F4 and the upper floats stay at the top, either cooling fits. The lower floats are at the bottom and just settling.\n\nF5: the upper floats came back down to the interface, which means the upper layer was heated (event 5 = Heat Upper). The F4 image at 25 s shows them still at the top, and event 5 started at 32 s. But F5 is at 31 s, which falls within event 4 (26 s start). So event 4 = Heat Upper. In F5 the lower floats are still at the bottom. F6 shows the lower floats rising slightly, so event 5 = Heat Lower. Event 6 would then be unclear. The lower floats in F6 are only slightly raised, so event 6 might be another Heat Lower.\n\nEvent 3 is then Cool Upper or Cool Lower. I choose Cool Lower.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["Cool", "LowerLayer"], "predicted": ["Cool", "LowerLayer"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["Cool", "UpperLayer"], "predicted": ["Cool", "UpperLayer"], "correct": true}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["Heat", "LowerLayer"], "predicted": ["Cool", "LowerLayer"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["Cool", "LowerLayer"], "predicted": ["Heat", "UpperLayer"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["Heat", "UpperLayer"], "predicted": ["Heat", "LowerLayer"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["Heat", "LowerLayer"], "predicted": ["Heat", "LowerLayer"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S07", "title": "Heated gas bulbs", "domain": "Thermal", "question_id": "scene_15/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nA fixed oblique camera shows three connected glass bulbs arranged left, middle, and right, each with a visible meniscus. Bulb envelopes, glass network, backboard and supports are fixed references. Warm-water contact caps are outside the interior view. Gas composition can vary between experiments; do not assume a sample-specific gas identity that is not visible. heat_bulb_1, heat_bulb_2 and heat_bulb_3 mean heating the left, middle and right bulb respectively. An event heats one or two bulbs simultaneously. The apparatus is continuous and thermal states are not reset.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event image shows the active response after one thermal-contact event. Compare each meniscus with its own glass envelope, accounting for the oblique view and retained thermal history. No unobserved gas assignment is supplied.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=1.966667 s; F1=7.300000 s; F2=12.800000 s; F3=18.300000 s; F4=23.800000 s; F5=29.300000 s; F6=34.800000 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nheat_bulb_1, heat_bulb_2, heat_bulb_3\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"heat_bulb_1\"],[\"heat_bulb_2\"],[\"heat_bulb_3\"],[\"heat_bulb_1\",\"heat_bulb_2\"],[\"heat_bulb_1\",\"heat_bulb_3\"],[\"heat_bulb_2\",\"heat_bulb_3\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"heat_bulb_1\"], [\"heat_bulb_2\"], [\"heat_bulb_3\"], [\"heat_bulb_1\", \"heat_bulb_2\"], [\"heat_bulb_1\", \"heat_bulb_3\"], [\"heat_bulb_2\", \"heat_bulb_3\"]].", "excerpt": "The right level rose (378 to 362), the left dropped (385 to 395) and the middle dropped (380 to 390). The right bulb is the most likely one heated, so I chose heat_bulb_3.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I could not read the meniscus levels reliably, so these six events are a best guess from rough estimates of each frame, not a confident reconstruction.\n\nApproximate meniscus top positions (y in pixels; a lower y means a higher level):\n\n| Frame | Left | Middle | Right |\n|-------|------|--------|-------|\n| F0 | 385 | 380 | 378 |\n| F1 | 395 | 390 | 362 |\n| F2 | 378 | 378 | 382 |\n| F3 | 378 | 362 | 395 |\n| F4 | 378 | 388 | 378 |\n| F5 | 368 | 398 | 380 |\n| F6 | 378 | 380 | 380 |\n\nEvent-by-event reading:\n\n- **F0→F1:** The right level rose (378 to 362), the left dropped (385 to 395) and the middle dropped (380 to 390). The right bulb is the most likely one heated, so I chose heat_bulb_3.\n- **F1→F2:** The left rose (395 to 378), the middle rose (390 to 378) and the right fell (362 to 382). I chose heat_bulb_1 and heat_bulb_2 together.\n- **F2→F3:** The middle rose (378 to 362), the right fell (382 to 395) and the left was unchanged. I chose heat_bulb_2.\n- **F3→F4:** The middle fell (362 to 388), the right rose (395 to 378) and the left was unchanged. I chose heat_bulb_3.\n- **F4→F5:** The left rose (378 to 368) and the middle fell (388 to 398). I chose heat_bulb_1.\n- **F5→F6:** The left fell (368 to 378), the middle rose (398 to 380) and the right was roughly unchanged. I chose heat_bulb_2.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["heat_bulb_1", "heat_bulb_2"], "predicted": ["heat_bulb_3"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["heat_bulb_3"], "predicted": ["heat_bulb_1", "heat_bulb_2"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["heat_bulb_1", "heat_bulb_3"], "predicted": ["heat_bulb_2"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["heat_bulb_2"], "predicted": ["heat_bulb_3"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["heat_bulb_2", "heat_bulb_3"], "predicted": ["heat_bulb_1"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["heat_bulb_1"], "predicted": ["heat_bulb_2"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S08", "title": "Bimetal supports", "domain": "Thermal", "question_id": "scene_16/sample_0004/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0004", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nApparatus and view: three radial bonded-metal strips surround a central brass annulus. The left-front strip has a lower layer of 6061-T6 aluminium, the right-front strip a lower layer of C110 half-hard copper, and the rear strip a lower layer of 304 stainless steel. All three have an Invar 36 upper layer bonded to the lower layer. Their outer ends are clamped to the fixed base; their inner free ends support the annulus through three small ceramic balls. The annulus and its ungraduated central pointer form one rigid assembly. A central sliding/tilting bearing allows its height and tilt to vary while keeping its centre horizontally located. The base and outer clamps remain fixed. The camera is inside the observation area, above and in front of the apparatus, with a small rightward offset; rear means farther from the camera. The strips, ceramic supports, ring and pointer are visible. The exposed metal finishes are fixed, not temperature colours.\n\nThree external radiant heaters stand to the left, behind, and to the right of the apparatus. Each is directed at the corresponding strip: left at the aluminium strip, rear at the stainless-steel strip, and right at the copper strip. The heaters and power sliders are outside the supplied interior view. The three lamps have identical nominal radiant output when fully on. The initial apparatus temperature is 22 degrees Celsius. Physical temperatures, positions and loads are not reset between events. Small installation-angle and radius differences can occur between experiments, but the installation stays fixed within one sequence.\n\nControls: SelectLeftLamp selects only the left heater; SelectRearLamp selects only the rear heater; SelectRightLamp selects only the right heater. SelectLeftLamp+SelectRearLamp, SelectLeftLamp+SelectRightLamp and SelectRearLamp+SelectRightLamp select the named two heaters simultaneously. Each event specifies the complete set of powered heaters; the other heater or heaters are off. The selected setting remains until the next event. Events may mix single-heater and two-heater settings.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event image is one actual frame from that event's 4.0-6.15 s response window, with the selected heater setting established. Event commands are 6.2 s apart. Temperatures and support/ring motion continue evolving.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=2.383333 s; F1=8.550000 s; F2=14.733335 s; F3=20.933334 s; F4=27.133335 s; F5=33.333336 s; F6=39.533337 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2.4 s, 8.6 s, 14.8 s, 21 s, 27.2 s, 33.4 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nSelectLeftLamp, SelectRearLamp, SelectRightLamp\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"SelectLeftLamp\"],[\"SelectRearLamp\"],[\"SelectRightLamp\"],[\"SelectLeftLamp\",\"SelectRearLamp\"],[\"SelectLeftLamp\",\"SelectRightLamp\"],[\"SelectRearLamp\",\"SelectRightLamp\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"SelectLeftLamp\"], [\"SelectRearLamp\"], [\"SelectRightLamp\"], [\"SelectLeftLamp\", \"SelectRearLamp\"], [\"SelectLeftLamp\", \"SelectRightLamp\"], [\"SelectRearLamp\", \"SelectRightLamp\"]].", "excerpt": "I assumed the pointer leans away from the strip that is heated most (its end rises most). The rear strip is farthest from the camera, and its rise would tip the pointer toward the rear (up in the image). Lifting the left strip would tilt the pointer toward the right and rear, and lifting the right strip would tilt it toward the left.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "This is a low-confidence guess. I read the pointer tip position and the height of the central hub in each frame, and I could not fully separate the three strips' contributions.\n\nPointer tip positions in the image, with the hub at about y=377:\n- F0: (463,168)\n- F1: (475,168)\n- F2: (487,163)\n- F3: (474,164)\n- F4: (477,156)\n- F5: (467,155)\n- F6: (473,148)\n\nThe pointer's height rises overall, from 168 to 148, so the heaters keep adding heat to the strips.\n\nTilt estimates, from tip x relative to the hub at about x=470:\n- F0: about −7, nearly untilted.\n- F1: +5.\n- F2: +17, the tip leans right.\n- F3: +4.\n- F4: +7.\n- F5: −3.\n- F6: +3.\n\nI assumed the pointer leans away from the strip that is heated most (its end rises most). The rear strip is farthest from the camera, and its rise would tip the pointer toward the rear (up in the image). Lifting the left strip would tilt the pointer toward the right and rear, and lifting the right strip would tilt it toward the left. Aluminium has the highest expansion, then copper, then stainless steel, so with the Invar layer the aluminium strip bends most, then copper, then steel.\n\nEvent assignments:\n- Event 1, F1: the tip moved right by 12, so the left strip is heated, giving Left.\n- Event 2, F2: the tip moved right again, with a slight rise, so Left continues or Left+Rear. I chose Left+Rear.\n- Event 3, F3: the tip moved back left by 13, so the right strip is heated, giving Right or Rear+Right. I chose Rear+Right.\n- Event 4, F4: the tip rose by 8 and moved right slightly, so Left+Rear.\n- Event 5, F5: the tip moved left by 10 with little height change, so Right.\n- Event 6, F6: the tip rose by 7 and moved right by 6, so Left+Rear.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["SelectLeftLamp", "SelectRearLamp"], "predicted": ["SelectLeftLamp"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["SelectLeftLamp"], "predicted": ["SelectLeftLamp", "SelectRearLamp"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["SelectRearLamp", "SelectRightLamp"], "predicted": ["SelectRearLamp", "SelectRightLamp"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["SelectLeftLamp", "SelectRightLamp"], "predicted": ["SelectLeftLamp", "SelectRearLamp"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["SelectRightLamp"], "predicted": ["SelectRightLamp"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["SelectLeftLamp", "SelectRightLamp"], "predicted": ["SelectLeftLamp", "SelectRearLamp"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S09", "title": "Magnetic coils", "domain": "Electromagnetism", "question_id": "scene_02/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS\nThe interior camera looks down at red-and-blue compass needles on brass pivots and fixed circular bases. The red end is NORTH and the blue end is SOUTH. Image-right is table +x; the far edge of the table is +z; upward from the tabletop is +y. The nominal compass slots form three columns at x=-2.60, 0 and +2.60 m and near/middle/far rows at z=-1.40, +0.45 and +2.30 m. Installed bases can have up to 0.06 m of horizontal placement variation. Some slots may be empty. The needle magnetic centres are at y=0.970 m. Base positions and needle identities remain fixed within an experiment.\n\nThree external electromagnets are outside the interior view. Coil1 is beyond the left-near side of the table, Coil2 is beyond the middle of the near edge, and Coil3 is beyond the right-near side. Their centres, in the same table coordinates, are approximately (-7.20, 2.42, -3.26), (0, 3.55, -5.95) and (+7.20, 2.42, -3.26) m. Their magnetic axes are horizontal. With Positive current, Coil1's north end points rightward and toward the far edge, along (0.84, 0, 0.55); Coil2's north end points toward the far edge, along (0, 0, 1); Coil3's north end points leftward and toward the far edge, along (-0.84, 0, 0.55). Negative current reverses the named coil's pole orientation. These are fixed winding and connection conventions, not observations of the hidden events.\n\nAll installed compass rotors have the same mechanical response: mass 0.12 kg, yaw inertia approximately 0.0164 kg m^2, angular damping 1.40/s and the same magnetic moment setting. The simulation uses three softened dipole sources with common distance softening 0.72 m and normalized-field scale 70. The common rotor torque scale is 0.030 N m per normalized field unit, with a field-magnitude cap of 2.4 units and a rotor angular-speed limit of 5.5 rad/s. Powered Coil1 and Coil3 approach current magnitude 2.00 A; Coil2 approaches 2.05 A. Current has a second-order electrical response with natural frequency 9.2/s and damping ratio 0.92. Positions, currents and residual needle motion are not reset between events.\n\nCONTROLS\nCoil1Positive and Coil1Negative energize Coil1 with the respective polarity. Coil2Positive and Coil2Negative do the same for Coil2; Coil3Positive and Coil3Negative do the same for Coil3. Exactly one coil is selected in each event; the other selectors remain in their unpowered neutral positions. Each selected contact is commanded for 2.4 s, followed by 3.2 s with all selectors neutral. Actual current and needle motion evolve continuously rather than being reset when a selector returns to neutral.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe recording starts with a 2.0 s initial hold. Each event image is a native interior frame from its powered response window. A contact is commanded for 2.4 s, followed by 3.2 s with selectors neutral; currents and compass motion continue evolving.\nCapture times (physical simulation clock; the F0 image precedes event 1): F1=4.133356 s; F2=9.733271 s; F3=15.333185 s; F4=20.933100 s; F5=26.533014 s; F6=32.132957 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2 s, 7.6 s, 13.2 s, 18.8 s, 24.4 s, 30 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nCoil1Positive, Coil1Negative, Coil2Positive, Coil2Negative, Coil3Positive, Coil3Negative\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"Coil1Positive\"],[\"Coil1Negative\"],[\"Coil2Positive\"],[\"Coil2Negative\"],[\"Coil3Positive\"],[\"Coil3Negative\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"Coil1Positive\"], [\"Coil1Negative\"], [\"Coil2Positive\"], [\"Coil2Negative\"], [\"Coil3Positive\"], [\"Coil3Negative\"]].", "excerpt": "The left column rotated strongly. The near-left needle now points red to the left and blue to the right. The middle-near needle also changed, with red toward the upper left. The other needles changed little. Coil1 sits on the left, so it is the likely source. Coil1 Positive would push north rightward and toward the far edge, but the red end went left, so this looks like Coil1Negative.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I could not work this out rigorously, so these are best guesses from rough visual cues.\n\n**F0 to F1.** The left column rotated strongly. The near-left needle now points red to the left and blue to the right. The middle-near needle also changed, with red toward the upper left. The other needles changed little. Coil1 sits on the left, so it is the likely source. Coil1 Positive would push north rightward and toward the far edge, but the red end went left, so this looks like Coil1Negative.\n\n**F1 to F2.** The left column changed again: the near-left needle now has blue toward the far edge and red toward the near edge. That means north points toward the near edge, which is the opposite of Coil2Positive's far-edge direction. The left-middle needle also flipped, with blue up and red down. The middle-near needle is still tilted red toward the upper left. This fits Coil2Negative, whose north end would point toward the near edge. A coil with a negative sign is the better fit here, though Coil1 and Coil2 are both plausible.\n\n**F2 to F3.** The right column changed: the right-middle needle now has red up and blue down, and the right-near needle has red to the upper left and blue to the lower right. That suggests Coil3, and the red end pointing upper left matches Coil3Positive.\n\n**F3 to F4.** Most needles flipped, with blue now pointing up in the left and middle columns and red toward the near edge. The near-middle needle has red pointing down. This suggests Coil2Negative, which would point north toward the near edge.\n\n**F4 to F5.** The near-middle needle has blue on the left and red on the right, so north points right. Coil1Positive pushes north rightward and toward the far edge, so this fits Coil1Positive.\n\n**F5 to F6.** The right-near needle has blue on the left and red on the right, so north points right. Coil3Negative would point north rightward and toward the near edge, so this fits Coil3Negative.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["Coil1Negative"], "predicted": ["Coil1Negative"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["Coil1Positive"], "predicted": ["Coil2Negative"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["Coil3Positive"], "predicted": ["Coil3Positive"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["Coil2Negative"], "predicted": ["Coil2Negative"], "correct": true}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["Coil2Positive"], "predicted": ["Coil1Positive"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["Coil3Negative"], "predicted": ["Coil3Negative"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S10", "title": "Electric dipoles", "domain": "Electromagnetism", "question_id": "scene_07/sample_0009/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0009", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS\nThe fixed camera looks at two horizontal rows; image-right is world +x and image-up is +y. The upper row is Dipole A and the lower row is Dipole B. Each row has a light probe on the left and a dark probe on the right, both sliding horizontally between fixed outer spring anchors. A and B are separate, spatially distinct dipoles. In each dipole, the copper electrode is on the left and the blue electrode is on the right. A small neutral ivory body is fixed between the rows. The dipoles and the neutral body remain part of the apparatus throughout the sequence.\n\nCALIBRATION FACTS FOR THIS APPARATUS\nAt the start of every sample, the hidden source magnitudes are A=37.5 microC and B=75.0 microC. A single source control changes A by 25.0 microC or B by 50.0 microC in the indicated direction; the sign of each source does not change. The four light probes have charge signs +, -, +, - from upper-left to lower-right. The two upper probes have mass 0.025 kg and spring damping 0.305 N s/m; the two lower probes have mass 0.550 kg and damping 1.20 N s/m. The neutral ivory body has mass 0.120 kg. These values are calibration information about the fixed apparatus, not labels for the hidden events.\n\nALLOWED LABELS\nIncreaseChargeA: increase the magnitude of upper Dipole A while preserving its electrode signs.\nDecreaseChargeA: decrease the magnitude of upper Dipole A while preserving its electrode signs.\nIncreaseChargeB: increase the magnitude of lower Dipole B while preserving its electrode signs.\nDecreaseChargeB: decrease the magnitude of lower Dipole B while preserving its electrode signs.\nAn event contains one label or one A label together with one B label. Do not add a label merely because a probe is still moving from an earlier event.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe evidence frame is taken about 3.5 s after its corresponding event, before the next one. Use fixed anchors, row identity, probe signs, relative displacement and persistent motion; one probe or colour alone does not identify an event.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=1.966667 s; F1=5.500000 s; F2=11.000000 s; F3=16.500000 s; F4=22.000000 s; F5=27.500000 s; F6=33.000000 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nIncreaseChargeA, DecreaseChargeA, IncreaseChargeB, DecreaseChargeB\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"IncreaseChargeA\"],[\"DecreaseChargeA\"],[\"IncreaseChargeB\"],[\"DecreaseChargeB\"],[\"IncreaseChargeA\",\"IncreaseChargeB\"],[\"DecreaseChargeB\",\"IncreaseChargeA\"],[\"DecreaseChargeA\",\"IncreaseChargeB\"],[\"DecreaseChargeA\",\"DecreaseChargeB\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"IncreaseChargeA\"], [\"DecreaseChargeA\"], [\"IncreaseChargeB\"], [\"DecreaseChargeB\"], [\"IncreaseChargeA\", \"IncreaseChargeB\"], [\"DecreaseChargeB\", \"IncreaseChargeA\"], [\"DecreaseChargeA\", \"IncreaseChargeB\"], [\"DecreaseChargeA\", \"DecreaseChargeB\"]].", "excerpt": "The upper pair moved apart slightly (gap 307 to 321), and the lower pair moved apart more (gap 287 to 347). Both dipoles weakened, but B changed more, so I take this as a decrease of both A and B.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I read each event from how far the probes moved between consecutive frames. In each row the left probe is light and the right probe is dark. The probes sit at their equilibrium positions, so closer together means stronger attraction. Probe positions are approximate pixel x-coordinates read from the images.\n\n**Positions per frame**\n\n| Frame | Upper left | Upper right | Lower left | Lower right |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F0 | 327 | 634 | 332 | 619 |\n| F1 | 320 | 641 | 302 | 649 |\n| F2 | 327 | 634 | 332 | 618 |\n| F3 | 320 | 641 | 302 | 649 |\n| F4 | 348 | 613 | 347 | 604 |\n| F5 | 394 | 567 | 396 | 555 |\n| F6 | 348 | 613 | 345 | 606 |\n\n**Reading the changes**\n\n- **F0 to F1:** The upper pair moved apart slightly (gap 307 to 321), and the lower pair moved apart more (gap 287 to 347). Both dipoles weakened, but B changed more, so I take this as a decrease of both A and B.\n- **F1 to F2:** Both rows returned to their F0 positions, so this is the reverse of the previous event: an increase of both A and B.\n- **F2 to F3:** The positions match F1 again, so this is a decrease of both A and B.\n- **F3 to F4:** The upper gap shrank to 265 and the lower gap to 257, both tighter than F0 (307 and 287). Relative to F3 that is a strong increase in both. Starting from A=12.5 and B=25 after F3, A rose to 37.5 or 62.5 and B rose to 75, which fits increasing both. A and B each get one step, so the pair is IncreaseChargeA with IncreaseChargeB.\n- **F4 to F5:** The upper gap shrank to 173 and the lower gap to 159, so both dipoles strengthened further. This is another increase of both.\n- **F5 to F6:** Both rows returned to F4-like positions, so both dipoles weakened. This is a decrease of both.\n\n**Checking the charge levels**\n\n- After F0, A=37.5 and B=75.\n- After event 1 (decrease both), A=12.5 and B=25.\n- After event 2 (increase both), A=37.5 and B=75, matching F0.\n- After event 3 (decrease both), A=12.5 and B=25, matching F1.\n- After event 4 (increase both), A=37.5 and B=75, which should match F0, but F4 is tighter than F0. This conflicts with the readings, so the F4 increase may be larger than one step, or the sequence differs from this simple model.\n- After event 5 (increase both), A=62.5 and B=125, and the probes are closer still, which is consistent with F5.\n- After event 6 (decrease both), A=37.5 and B=75, but F6 is nearly the same as F4, which is also inconsistent with F0.\n\nThe F4 and F6 positions agree with each other, and F4 differs from F0 by a consistent offset. That offset is probably a persistent-motion or neutral-body effect, so I keep the sequence as read from the frame-to-frame changes.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["DecreaseChargeB", "IncreaseChargeA"], "predicted": ["DecreaseChargeA", "DecreaseChargeB"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["DecreaseChargeA", "IncreaseChargeB"], "predicted": ["IncreaseChargeA", "IncreaseChargeB"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["DecreaseChargeB", "IncreaseChargeA"], "predicted": ["DecreaseChargeA", "DecreaseChargeB"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["IncreaseChargeB"], "predicted": ["IncreaseChargeA", "IncreaseChargeB"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["IncreaseChargeA", "IncreaseChargeB"], "predicted": ["IncreaseChargeA", "IncreaseChargeB"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["DecreaseChargeA", "DecreaseChargeB"], "predicted": ["DecreaseChargeA", "DecreaseChargeB"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S11", "title": "Dielectric response", "domain": "Electromagnetism", "question_id": "scene_13/sample_0007/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0007", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS AND VIEW\nOutside the receiver is a charged, isolated parallel-plate capacitor. One intact conducting wire connects its charged terminal to the electroscope stem, crosshead, two narrow conductive gold necks and the two gold leaves. The counter-electrode is grounded. The isolated charged assembly is not connected to a battery during the sequence. Its positive initial total charge is about 27.83 nanocoulombs. A very small passive leakage through 5e15 ohms is present, not a charge reset or a separately selected operation.\n\nOne actual pair of gold leaves hangs from the crosshead inside the glazed cabinet: the left leaf is attached to the left neck and the right leaf to the right neck. Gravity points downward. Both leaves carry charge of the same sign and open away from the centre; opening angle means each leaf's deflection from its own downward vertical, not an image-space rotation of the camera. The gold colour does not encode an operation. The brass frame, graphite backdrop, fixed illumination and glass walls are passive and unchanged during a sample. There are no answer indicators, numerical scales or event-dependent marker colours.\n\nThe interior camera is physically inside the front observation window and looks inward toward both leaves and the opaque rear backdrop. Image-up is approximately laboratory-up; the view is not mirror-reversed. Its physical mounting can vary between samples by up to 8 degrees horizontally and from -3 to +5 degrees in elevation. The mounting and an 80-degree vertical field of view stay fixed throughout each sample. Both complete leaf envelopes and attachment points remain in view. The external carriage is blocked by real opaque cabinet surfaces, not another hidden input. Use each leaf relative to its own attachment and compare the two sides; modest perspective can make their projected lengths or deflections unequal.\n\nPHYSICAL COUPLING\nThe capacitor has plate area 0.002304 square metres and an 0.008-metre air gap. An 0.0068-metre-thick mica slab, relative permittivity 4.8, slides sideways into that gap without contacting either plate. Its plate-overlap fraction is u: 0 when fully withdrawn, 0.5 for half coverage and 1 for full coverage. The capacitance is the parallel sum of uncovered and mica-covered areas:\nCplate = epsilon0*A*((1-u)/gap + u/(gap-thickness+thickness/4.8)).\nThere is also 2.8 picofarads of fixed stray capacitance and a small, continuous contribution from the two actual leaf configurations. Increasing actual dielectric overlap increases capacitance. Because charge is approximately retained rather than held at constant voltage, this tends to lower voltage and electrostatic opening force; withdrawing tends to do the reverse. The mechanical response is not instantaneous: gold-strip elasticity, gravity, actual foil inertia and passive damping determine the subsequent leaf motion. Opening depends on the evolving charge/voltage and preceding mechanical state, not simply the name of the most recent command.\n\nThe leaves are thin gold foils, approximately 6 by 39.2 millimetres, 0.36 micrometres thick and about 1.636 milligrams each, attached through 6-millimetre-wide, 2-millimetre-long conductive necks of the same thickness. Gold density is 19,320 kilograms per cubic metre; Young's modulus is 77.91 gigapascals. Gravity is 9.81 metres per second squared. This apparatus uses a declared reduced, inextensible one-coordinate gold-flexure energy model with mass and inertia measured from its actual rendered foil mesh, geometric electrostatic work, gravity, elasticity and a fixed passive air-loss surrogate. It is not a calibrated three-dimensional field solution or full foil-shell FEM. No operation-to-leaf-angle lookup table drives the response.\n\nALLOWED COMMANDS\nReturn exactly one of these labels per event:\nInsertHalf: set the single carriage so mica covers half of the capacitor plate area.\nInsertFully: set it so mica covers the full plate area.\nWithdrawDielectric: pull it completely clear, leaving zero plate overlap.\nThese are absolute physical endpoints, not relative increments. Half coverage is not half of the carriage's complete clearance travel. There is only one carriage, so simultaneously selecting two different endpoints is not a physically permitted double operation. Initially the slab is fully withdrawn. Every event changes the preceding endpoint; the first event therefore cannot be WithdrawDielectric. A type can recur after another type. The carriage moves at 0.52 scene units per second (0.0208 metres per second); the full clearance travel is 1.30 scene units, at most 2.5 seconds. It then holds its selected position.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe first command follows a one-second initial hold. Each event lasts 6.1 s. The selected native image lies 4.5-6.0 s after that event begins, after carriage travel and at low leaf angular speed. A common label-blind rule selects the largest two-leaf tip displacement in that window relative to the actual pre-event image. Compare each leaf relative to its own attachment and preserve the inferred endpoint history.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=0.966667 s; F1=7.000000 s; F2=11.600000 s; F3=19.166666 s; F4=23.833334 s; F5=31.400000 s; F6=36.000000 s.\nSupplied event start times: 1 s, 7.1 s, 13.2 s, 19.3 s, 25.4 s, 31.5 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nInsertHalf, InsertFully, WithdrawDielectric\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"InsertHalf\"],[\"InsertFully\"],[\"WithdrawDielectric\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"InsertHalf\"], [\"InsertFully\"], [\"WithdrawDielectric\"]].", "excerpt": "The leaf opening angle in each frame identifies the dielectric endpoint, since more mica means higher capacitance, lower voltage and a smaller opening.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "The leaf opening angle in each frame identifies the dielectric endpoint, since more mica means higher capacitance, lower voltage and a smaller opening.\n\n**Leaf states from the images**\n- F0, 0 coverage: wide opening (tips near y=295).\n- F1: nearly closed (tips near y=460), so the most coverage, which is full.\n- F2: identical to F0, so withdrawn.\n- F3: intermediate (tips near y=410), which is half coverage.\n- F4: identical to F0, so withdrawn.\n- F5: identical to F3, so half.\n- F6: identical to F0, so withdrawn.\n\n**Event sequence**\n1. F0 to F1: the slab goes from withdrawn to full, so InsertFully.\n2. F1 to F2: back to the F0 state, so WithdrawDielectric.\n3. F2 to F3: intermediate opening, so InsertHalf.\n4. F3 to F4: back to wide opening, so WithdrawDielectric.\n5. F4 to F5: intermediate opening, so InsertHalf.\n6. F5 to F6: back to wide opening, so WithdrawDielectric.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["InsertFully"], "predicted": ["InsertFully"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["WithdrawDielectric"], "predicted": ["WithdrawDielectric"], "correct": true}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["InsertHalf"], "predicted": ["InsertHalf"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["WithdrawDielectric"], "predicted": ["WithdrawDielectric"], "correct": true}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["InsertHalf"], "predicted": ["InsertHalf"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["WithdrawDielectric"], "predicted": ["WithdrawDielectric"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": true}, {"id": "S12", "title": "Conducting wires", "domain": "Electromagnetism", "question_id": "scene_14/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nApparatus and view: three parallel, horizontal hollow copper conductors hang from pairs of nonmagnetic rods. A has red collars and leads, B amber-yellow collars and leads, and C blue collars and leads. These colours identify the conductor, not current polarity. The conductors occupy three left/middle/right installation slots, spaced 0.12 m apart; their suspension heights differ by increments of 0.009 m. Identity-to-slot and identity-to-height assignments can vary between samples, but remain fixed within one sample. The camera looks obliquely from the front at the suspended bars and their supports; image-right is approximately laboratory right and image-up approximately upward. The external switchboard is to the left of the apparatus and is not included in the supplied interior images. Each bar has two real flexible copper-braid connections to its closed circuit. The overhead anchors, support frame and background fixtures are fixed parts. The small colours and material finishes are constant.\n\nEach copper bar is 0.235 m long, and each suspension rod is 0.162 m long. A selected pair has a 350 A supply preset when its initial centre-to-centre distance is at most 0.18 m, otherwise 500 A; the same magnitude rule applies to both relative current directions. The external isolated supplies have 8 V compliance and 3 mH series inductance. A branch whose supply is not selected remains connected through a passive 0.001 ohm flyback load; it is not an open circuit. These are lumped circuit elements of the simulated apparatus. The experiment does not reset its conductor positions, velocities, currents or temperatures between events.\n\nControls: ABSame selects the A and B supplies with their currents commanded in the same direction along the two bars; ABOpposite selects A and B with opposite directions. BCSame and BCOpposite do the same for B and C; ACSame and ACOpposite for A and C. Each event chooses one of these six complete settings. The actual contacts change over before establishing the newly selected sources.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event image is the native interior frame nearest 2.00 s into its event period, after selected contacts and currents are established. Inherited bar motion, currents and temperature remain physical state, so the image need not be a fresh equilibrium.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=0.333333 s; F1=2.000000 s; F2=5.600000 s; F3=9.200000 s; F4=15.200000 s; F5=18.799999 s; F6=24.799999 s.\nDurations of the supplied event periods: 3.6 s, 3.6 s, 6 s, 3.6 s, 6 s, 9.6 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nABSame, ABOpposite, BCSame, BCOpposite, ACSame, ACOpposite\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"ABSame\"],[\"ABOpposite\"],[\"BCSame\"],[\"BCOpposite\"],[\"ACSame\"],[\"ACOpposite\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"ABSame\"], [\"ABOpposite\"], [\"BCSame\"], [\"BCOpposite\"], [\"ACSame\"], [\"ACOpposite\"]].", "excerpt": "Blue's bottom end moves slightly right, amber's bottom end moves slightly left, and red barely changes. Blue and amber moving toward each other would mean attraction in a same-direction BC pair, so I chose BCSame.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I could not extract precise motion data from the images, so this sequence is a best-supported guess rather than a firm reconstruction.\n\nThe slot order is blue (C) on the left, amber (B) in the middle, and red (A) on the right.\n\nWhat I could read from the frames:\n\n- **F0 to F1:** Blue's bottom end moves slightly right, amber's bottom end moves slightly left, and red barely changes. Blue and amber moving toward each other would mean attraction in a same-direction BC pair, so I chose BCSame.\n- **F1 to F2:** Red's end moves left, and its rods lean left at the bottom. Blue and amber look roughly unchanged. A red move toward amber suggests attraction in ABSame.\n- **F2 to F3:** Red moves back right, while blue and amber move back left. Blue returning left, away from amber, suggests repulsion, so I chose BCOpposite. Red moving right, away from amber, would also fit ABOpposite, but BCOpposite also accounts for the blue motion, so I chose it.\n- **F3 to F4:** Blue moves right and amber moves right and forward, with its rods swinging. Red is stable. I could not separate the candidates, so I chose BCSame.\n- **F4 to F5:** Red moves left, and amber and blue change little. I chose ABSame.\n- **F5 to F6:** Red returns right and blue returns slightly right. I chose ABOpposite.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["BCSame"], "predicted": ["BCSame"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["ACSame"], "predicted": ["ABSame"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["ABOpposite"], "predicted": ["BCOpposite"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["BCOpposite"], "predicted": ["BCSame"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["ABSame"], "predicted": ["ABSame"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["ACOpposite"], "predicted": ["ABOpposite"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S13", "title": "Standing waves", "domain": "Acoustics", "question_id": "scene_05/sample_0030/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0030", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS\nA horizontal transparent tube contains loose cork granules along its bottom. The tube is 1.80 m long with an internal radius of 0.105 m. Its left end has an externally driven diaphragm; its right end is closed by a fixed piston. The camera looks into the tube from the front side. The external frequency wheel and diaphragm-drive lever are outside this view. No piston or camera movement is a control in this experiment.\n\nA microphone samples the tube near the right end, approximately five-sixths of the distance from the left diaphragm. The black powered RMS detector and its orange spring-loaded indicator are visible on the right/front side of the tube. The orange marker travels vertically on its fixed black rail. The detector converts local RMS sound pressure into coil force with a gain of 0.00066 N/Pa; the return spring is 18 N/m. The indicator has 0.12 m of travel, with zero at its lower rest position. Air density is 1.2 kg/m^3 and nominal sound speed is 343 m/s. The simulated tube uses a uniform linear acoustic momentum-loss rate of 150/s and a 0.115 s field-envelope relaxation. These are apparatus/model parameters, not additional hidden controls. Particle sizes, initial pour and initial frequency may differ between experiments. Positions and physical states are not reset between events.\n\nCONTROLS\nIncreaseFrequency moves the external frequency wheel one setting higher; DecreaseFrequency moves it one setting lower. Its three allowed frequency settings are 381.11, 476.39 and 571.67 Hz. IncreaseDrive moves the external drive lever one setting higher, increasing diaphragm displacement amplitude; DecreaseDrive moves it one setting lower. The three displacement-amplitude settings are 0.420, 0.733 and 1.150 mm. An event may apply one control or two controls simultaneously. Infer the complete set of controls for each event.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event occupies 6.1 s, including 1.1 s for control motion and 5.0 s for observation. Its native evidence frame comes from the mature response window. Residual particle motion and sound-envelope response can persist.\nCapture times (physical simulation clock; the F0 image precedes event 1): F1=4.600000 s; F2=10.700000 s; F3=16.800001 s; F4=22.900000 s; F5=29.000000 s; F6=35.100002 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nIncreaseFrequency, DecreaseFrequency, IncreaseDrive, DecreaseDrive\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"IncreaseFrequency\"],[\"DecreaseFrequency\"],[\"IncreaseDrive\"],[\"DecreaseDrive\"],[\"IncreaseDrive\",\"IncreaseFrequency\"],[\"DecreaseDrive\",\"IncreaseFrequency\"],[\"DecreaseFrequency\",\"IncreaseDrive\"],[\"DecreaseDrive\",\"DecreaseFrequency\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"IncreaseFrequency\"], [\"DecreaseFrequency\"], [\"IncreaseDrive\"], [\"DecreaseDrive\"], [\"IncreaseDrive\", \"IncreaseFrequency\"], [\"DecreaseDrive\", \"IncreaseFrequency\"], [\"DecreaseFrequency\", \"IncreaseDrive\"], [\"DecreaseDrive\", \"DecreaseFrequency\"]].", "excerpt": "F1 to F2: the harmonic drops to 5, so the frequency decreases. The marker is slightly lower, so I take the drive as unchanged, giving DecreaseFrequency alone. Two decreases would reach the lowest drive setting, which fits the flat cork.", "excerpt_event": 2, "response_text": "The tube resonates at f_n = n·343/(2·1.8) = n·95.28 Hz. The three frequency settings are therefore the 4th, 5th and 6th harmonics (381.11, 476.39 and 571.67 Hz). The number of cork piles tells the harmonic, because there is one pile per half wavelength.\n\n**Pile counts (the pile at each end counts):**\n- F0 shows about 7 piles, at x ≈ 30, 185, 335, 485, 635, 780, 930. This matches the 6th harmonic (571.67 Hz), which has 6 intervals and 7 nodes.\n- F1 shows piles at 55, 210, 395, 570, 750, 915, so also about 6–7 piles. The spacing is about 175 px, which is still the 6th harmonic.\n- F2 shows piles at 50, 255, 483, 705, 920. That is 5 piles with spacing of about 225 px, which is the 5th harmonic (476.39 Hz).\n- F3 shows piles at 60, 220, 395, 565, 745, 918. That is 6 piles, so the 6th harmonic again.\n- F4 shows piles at 40, 210, 390, 570, 750, 920. That is still the 6th harmonic.\n- F5 shows piles at 40, 180, 330, 480, 630, 785, 930. The spacing is about 150 px, which is a higher harmonic and would be 7 if it existed, so I read it as the 6th with finer spacing.\n- F6 looks the same as F5.\n\nI treat the 6th harmonic as the top setting, so the frequency pattern is 6, 6, 5, 6, 6, 6. This is inconsistent at F1 versus F0, so the readings are uncertain. The simplest sequence is F0 at 571.67 Hz, F1 unchanged, F2 down to 476.39 Hz, F3 back up, and F4–F6 unchanged.\n\n**Indicator height (orange marker):** F0 is at 155, F1 at 207, F2 at 214, F3 at 207, F4 at 181, F5 at 104, F6 at 155. A lower marker means lower amplitude, and the particles are flat in F1–F3, so the drive is low there.\n\n**Events:**\n1. F0 to F1: the marker drops and the cork flattens, so the drive decreases. The frequency is unchanged, giving DecreaseDrive.\n2. F1 to F2: the harmonic drops to 5, so the frequency decreases. The marker is slightly lower, so I take the drive as unchanged, giving DecreaseFrequency alone. Two decreases would reach the lowest drive setting, which fits the flat cork.\n3. F2 to F3: the frequency goes back up, giving IncreaseFrequency.\n4. F3 to F4: the particles become active and the marker rises, so the drive increases, giving IncreaseDrive.\n5. F4 to F5: the marker rises strongly, so the drive increases again, giving IncreaseDrive.\n6. F5 to F6: the marker drops and the particles are similar, so the drive decreases, giving DecreaseDrive.\n\nEvent 1 is the weakest call. It sets the drive to the lowest setting, but events 4 and 5 both raise it, which fits only if event 1 started from the middle setting.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["DecreaseDrive", "DecreaseFrequency"], "predicted": ["DecreaseDrive"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["DecreaseFrequency", "IncreaseDrive"], "predicted": ["DecreaseFrequency"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["DecreaseDrive", "IncreaseFrequency"], "predicted": ["IncreaseFrequency"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["IncreaseDrive"], "predicted": ["IncreaseDrive"], "correct": true}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["IncreaseDrive", "IncreaseFrequency"], "predicted": ["IncreaseDrive"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["DecreaseDrive"], "predicted": ["DecreaseDrive"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S14", "title": "Wave scattering", "domain": "Acoustics", "question_id": "scene_10/sample_0002/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0002", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS\nA fixed overhead camera views a rectangular blue-grey elastic-wave surface, 5.4 m wide and 4.6 m from near to far. Image-right is +x and image-up is +z. Two to four stationary cylindrical columns stand inside it. The surface is a damped elastic membrane represented by a scalar wave model, not a liquid flow simulation. Its calibrated propagation speed is 1.8 m/s and velocity-damping coefficient is 0.08 per second; its rim and column boundaries are reflective. The interior camera shows the surface, rim and columns. Four mechanical strikers are outside the left, right, near/bottom and far/top edges; they are outside the interior camera view. Each strike applies one short inward mechanical pulse lasting approximately 0.4 s. The surface is not reset between events.\n\nCONTROLS\npulse_left: activate the striker at the left edge.\npulse_right: activate the striker at the right edge.\npulse_front: activate the striker at the near/bottom edge.\npulse_back: activate the striker at the far/top edge.\nAn event may activate one striker or two strikers simultaneously. Return all activated controls for each event.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nCommands are spaced 5.0 s apart. Each event image is the first native 30-fps interior frame at least 1.4 s after the latest actual striker contact in that event and before the next event. These are post-contact response images, not images taken at the instant of impact.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=2.333333 s; F1=4.200000 s; F2=9.233334 s; F3=14.233334 s; F4=19.233334 s; F5=24.233334 s; F6=29.233334 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2.4 s, 7.4 s, 12.4 s, 17.4 s, 22.4 s, 27.4 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\npulse_left, pulse_right, pulse_front, pulse_back\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"pulse_left\"],[\"pulse_right\"],[\"pulse_front\"],[\"pulse_back\"],[\"pulse_back\",\"pulse_front\"],[\"pulse_back\",\"pulse_left\"],[\"pulse_back\",\"pulse_right\"],[\"pulse_front\",\"pulse_left\"],[\"pulse_front\",\"pulse_right\"],[\"pulse_left\",\"pulse_right\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"pulse_left\"], [\"pulse_right\"], [\"pulse_front\"], [\"pulse_back\"], [\"pulse_back\", \"pulse_front\"], [\"pulse_back\", \"pulse_left\"], [\"pulse_back\", \"pulse_right\"], [\"pulse_front\", \"pulse_left\"], [\"pulse_front\", \"pulse_right\"], [\"pulse_left\", \"pulse_right\"]].", "excerpt": "A pulse from the left edge would give vertical fronts about 2.5 m in from the left edge. A bottom pulse would give horizontal fronts. The curved bands that appear to come from the left and the bottom-left suggest both left and front, but this is uncertain.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I could only partly read the images, so most of these answers are best guesses.\n\nF1 is the only frame with clear wave fronts. They are curved bands that appear to originate from the left and the bottom-left, and a bright band also runs along the top-right. F1 is captured 1.8 s after the event start, which is about 1.4 s after the pulse ends. At 1.8 m/s, the wave has travelled roughly 2.5 m.\n\nA pulse from the left edge would give vertical fronts about 2.5 m in from the left edge. A bottom pulse would give horizontal fronts. The curved bands that appear to come from the left and the bottom-left suggest both left and front, but this is uncertain.\n\nF2 onward is too complex to decode. I fell back on plausible, varied combinations for events 2 to 6.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["pulse_back", "pulse_left"], "predicted": ["pulse_front", "pulse_left"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["pulse_front"], "predicted": ["pulse_right"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["pulse_back"], "predicted": ["pulse_back"], "correct": true}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["pulse_back", "pulse_right"], "predicted": ["pulse_left", "pulse_right"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["pulse_front", "pulse_right"], "predicted": ["pulse_front"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["pulse_back", "pulse_right"], "predicted": ["pulse_back", "pulse_left"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S15", "title": "Doppler response", "domain": "Acoustics", "question_id": "scene_19/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nApparatus: a siren mounted on a rail cart emits a steady 100 Hz tone. A stationary receiving horn is beyond one end of the straight rail; the cart stays on the same side of this horn throughout the experiment. The cart, rail and control lever are outside the interior camera view. The camera faces a fixed listening cabinet containing either 9 or 11 vertical, narrow-band indicator rods with orange paper flags, fixed bottom supports and a back wall. Rod heights are not ordered from left to right; alternate supports occupy slightly different depths. Count the installed rods in the initial image. From shortest rod to longest rod, a 9-rod bank is calibrated at 100.8, 100.6, 100.4, 100.2, 100.0, 99.8, 99.6, 99.4, 99.2 Hz; an 11-rod bank is calibrated at 101.0, 100.8, 100.6, 100.4, 100.2, 100.0, 99.8, 99.6, 99.4, 99.2, 99.0 Hz. Do not assume frequency order follows left-to-right screen position. These are calibrated amplitude indicators: their slow, camera-visible motion displays each channel's response envelope, rather than literal 100 Hz displacement. There is no audio input. The cart is stationary before the first event. The apparatus state is not reset between events.\n\nExternal controls: each event selects exactly one of four cart settings. SlowApproach means low-speed travel toward the receiving horn; FastApproach means high-speed travel toward the horn; SlowRecede means low-speed travel away from the horn; FastRecede means high-speed travel away from the horn. Low and high nominal speeds are approximately 0.64 and 2.15 m/s. The source tone itself is unchanged.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe initial image is taken before the first cart setting; the cart is initially stationary. Each later image is one native response frame for the next cart setting. Indicator rods display slow response envelopes, with previous envelope motion retained. No audio or exterior cart image is supplied.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=2.383333 s; F1=8.533334 s; F2=14.433334 s; F3=18.566668 s; F4=26.933334 s; F5=30.916668 s; F6=37.116669 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nSlowApproach, FastApproach, FastRecede, SlowRecede\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"SlowApproach\"],[\"FastApproach\"],[\"FastRecede\"],[\"SlowRecede\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"SlowApproach\"], [\"FastApproach\"], [\"FastRecede\"], [\"SlowRecede\"]].", "excerpt": "F1: rod 6 leaned strongly, and rods 7 and 4 moved a little. Rod 6 is at about 100.2 Hz by my assignment, which fits SlowApproach.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I counted 9 rods in F0, so the bank is the 9-rod calibration.\n\n**Rod heights (flag height, tallest to shortest).** Rod 9 (x≈715) and rod 8 (x≈650) have the highest flags, so they are the shortest rods. Rod 1 (x≈240) has a low flag, so it is the tallest rod.\n\nRod positions from left to right, with the frequency I assigned from flag height:\n- Rod 1 (flag y≈160): the lowest flag, so the longest rod, 99.2 Hz.\n- Rod 2 (y≈152): 99.4 Hz.\n- Rod 3 (y≈155): 99.6 Hz.\n- Rod 4 (y≈168): 99.8 Hz.\n- Rod 5 (y≈162): 100.0 Hz.\n- Rod 6 (y≈172): 100.2 Hz.\n- Rod 7 (y≈173): 100.4 Hz.\n- Rod 8 (y≈145): 100.6 Hz.\n- Rod 9 (y≈147): 100.8 Hz.\n\nThese assignments are rough. Heights run from rod 9 (shortest) to rod 1 (tallest), but the flag heights are too close to separate cleanly. I also guessed that rods 6, 4 and 5 sit at 100.2, 99.8 and 100.0 Hz.\n\n**Events.** Approach raises the received frequency above 100 Hz. Recede lowers it. Speed sets the size of the shift, using f ≈ 100·(1 ± v/343):\n- Slow (0.64 m/s): about ±0.19 Hz, so 100.2 or 99.8 Hz.\n- Fast (2.15 m/s): about ±0.63 Hz, so 100.6 or 99.4 Hz.\n\nWhich rod moved in each frame:\n- F1: rod 6 leaned strongly, and rods 7 and 4 moved a little. Rod 6 is at about 100.2 Hz by my assignment, which fits SlowApproach.\n- F2: rod 5 leaned, which I read as roughly 100.0 Hz. That does not fit a moving cart cleanly, but the rod 6 response is decaying. Rod 4 is also tilted slightly, so I take this as SlowRecede at about 99.8 Hz.\n- F3: rod 3 leaned strongly. Rod 3 is near 99.6 Hz, which I read as FastRecede at about 99.4 Hz.\n- F4: rod 1 leaned right and rod 5 leaned. Rod 1 is the tallest, at about 99.2 Hz, which fits a recede event. Rod 1 is at the 99.4 Hz end, so I read this as FastRecede.\n- F5: F5 looks the same as F3, with rod 3 leaning again, so I read it as FastRecede.\n- F6: rod 9 (the shortest, about 100.8 Hz) tilted most, so I read it as FastApproach.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["FastApproach"], "predicted": ["SlowApproach"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["SlowApproach"], "predicted": ["SlowRecede"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["SlowRecede"], "predicted": ["FastRecede"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["SlowApproach"], "predicted": ["FastRecede"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["SlowRecede"], "predicted": ["FastRecede"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["FastRecede"], "predicted": ["FastApproach"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S16", "title": "Two-source ripples", "domain": "Acoustics", "question_id": "scene_20/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nApparatus: a rectangular 4.8 m by 3.6 m ripple tank contains two fixed, circular, vertically moving plungers. Node A is the left-hand blue plunger and node B is the right-hand orange plunger in the overhead interior view; their positions and body colours do not exchange. Each plunger is connected to its own supported external actuator. Two external knobs set the phase of A and the phase difference B minus A. The knobs and actuator housings are outside the interior view. Both nodes run at 1.00 Hz with 0.021 m displacement amplitude. This is a calibrated linear surface-wave analogue with wave speed 0.66 m/s, damping and reflecting tank boundaries, not a calibrated simulation of sound propagating through water. The surrounding surface uses one continuous signed-height colour display: orange/brown is above mean height, blue is below, and grey is near mean height. Colours do not identify control labels. The state is not reset between events.\n\nControls: every event sets two values simultaneously. PhaseA_0deg, PhaseA_90deg and PhaseA_180deg set A's absolute phase offset to 0, 90 and 180 degrees. DeltaBA_0deg, DeltaBA_90deg and DeltaBA_180deg set B's phase minus A's phase to 0, 90 and 180 degrees. Either value may equal its previous setting. To specify the phase convention, after the actuator's 1.00 s smooth transition its vertical displacement is y_A(t)=0.021 sin(2*pi*t+phi_A), y_B(t)=0.021 sin(2*pi*t+phi_A+delta_BA), in metres, with upward positive, angles in radians in these formulas, and t the global elapsed time in seconds. The phase clock is not restarted at event boundaries. These formulas define the actuator commands, not the water-surface response.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe initial image is the unpowered flat surface at physical time zero. Commands are 6.0 s apart. Each event image is sampled at physical time 5.64 s after its command begins. The phase formulas use the global physical clock printed below, never a clock restarted at event boundaries. The wave field retains its physical history.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=0.000000 s; F1=7.640000 s; F2=13.640000 s; F3=19.639999 s; F4=25.639999 s; F5=31.639999 s; F6=37.639999 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2 s, 8 s, 14 s, 20 s, 26 s, 32 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nPhaseA_0deg, PhaseA_90deg, PhaseA_180deg, DeltaBA_0deg, DeltaBA_90deg, DeltaBA_180deg\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"DeltaBA_0deg\",\"PhaseA_0deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_90deg\",\"PhaseA_0deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_180deg\",\"PhaseA_0deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_0deg\",\"PhaseA_90deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_90deg\",\"PhaseA_90deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_180deg\",\"PhaseA_90deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_0deg\",\"PhaseA_180deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_90deg\",\"PhaseA_180deg\"],[\"DeltaBA_180deg\",\"PhaseA_180deg\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"DeltaBA_0deg\", \"PhaseA_0deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_90deg\", \"PhaseA_0deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_180deg\", \"PhaseA_0deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_0deg\", \"PhaseA_90deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_90deg\", \"PhaseA_90deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_180deg\", \"PhaseA_90deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_0deg\", \"PhaseA_180deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_90deg\", \"PhaseA_180deg\"], [\"DeltaBA_180deg\", \"PhaseA_180deg\"]].", "excerpt": "F1: A is dark blue and B is orange, so they are roughly opposite. This suggests δ=180 with A at φ=0 (A = −0.77, B = +0.77), so event 1 is DeltaBA_180deg with PhaseA_0deg.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I could not measure phases from the images, so this answer is a rough estimate from the colour patterns near each plunger, not a derived result.\n\nEach frame is sampled 5.64 s after its command, which is 0.64 s into the next cycle, so the phase at capture is 2π·0.64 plus the command phase. The 1.00 s transition has finished by then.\n\nPlunger displacement is sin(2πt + φ), with 2π·0.64 = 230.4°. The sine of 230.4° plus φ gives the height at the plunger:\n- φ=0: sin(230.4°) = −0.77, so below the mean (blue).\n- φ=90: sin(320.4°) = −0.64, so below the mean.\n- φ=180: sin(50.4°) = +0.77, so above the mean (orange).\n- φ=270 would give sin(140.4°) = +0.64, but it is not available.\n\nFor B, the phase is φ_A + δ.\n\nReadings by frame (colour at the plunger centre):\n- F1: A is dark blue and B is orange, so they are roughly opposite. This suggests δ=180 with A at φ=0 (A = −0.77, B = +0.77), so event 1 is DeltaBA_180deg with PhaseA_0deg.\n- F2: A is orange and B is dark blue. A is +, so φ_A=180 (+0.77), and B is at 360°, which is 0 and would read near-mean. B is dark, so this is not a clean fit. Alternatively φ_A=90 with δ=90 gives B = 180+230 = 50.4° → +0.77, which does not fit either. I will take A=180 with δ=180 as the best guess, giving B = −0.77.\n- F3: A is small and grey-blue, near zero, while B is also small and dark. This is hard to read, so I guess A=90, δ=90, which gives A = −0.64 and B = +0.77 (B is not clearly orange, so this is weak).\n- F4: both are orange, so the phases are about equal and positive. I take φ=180 with δ=0.\n- F5: A is orange and B is grey with an orange ring around it, so B is near zero. I take A=180 with δ=90, which gives B = sin(140.4°) = +0.64.\n- F6: A is grey (small) and B is dark blue. I take A=90 with δ=180 as a guess, which gives B = sin(50.4°+180°) = −0.77.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["DeltaBA_180deg", "PhaseA_180deg"], "predicted": ["DeltaBA_180deg", "PhaseA_0deg"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["DeltaBA_180deg", "PhaseA_0deg"], "predicted": ["DeltaBA_180deg", "PhaseA_180deg"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["DeltaBA_180deg", "PhaseA_90deg"], "predicted": ["DeltaBA_90deg", "PhaseA_90deg"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["DeltaBA_0deg", "PhaseA_0deg"], "predicted": ["DeltaBA_0deg", "PhaseA_180deg"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["DeltaBA_90deg", "PhaseA_0deg"], "predicted": ["DeltaBA_90deg", "PhaseA_180deg"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["DeltaBA_90deg", "PhaseA_90deg"], "predicted": ["DeltaBA_180deg", "PhaseA_90deg"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S17", "title": "Photoelastic load", "domain": "Optics", "question_id": "scene_04/sample_0007/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0007", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nThe complete optical calibration module is mounted 0.50 m forward of its original backplate plane so that the opaque loading beams do not block it. This is a fixed physical installation, not a display overlay or selectively hidden beam. The camera framing includes this upper reference and all three specimens.\n\nApparatus and camera. A fixed camera inside the enclosure faces the rear optical bench nearly straight on, from a slightly elevated position. Apparatus +x is image-right, +y is upward, and +z points away from the camera. Three separate framed transparent specimens stand between upper and lower loading pads: a narrow-waisted dogbone, an arch with two legs, and a circular disk with a central hole. All three are present, but their left-to-right order varies between samples. Identify them by shape in F0 rather than assuming a fixed order. A shared upper press crosshead moves their upper pads; their lower pads remain on the fixed anvil. A fourth, narrow horizontal optical calibration strip is mounted above the crosshead, centred over the specimens. It is not a fourth specimen under the press. Brass frames, the dark backplate, the bench, and fixed guide columns provide stationary references.\n\nThe optical arrangement uses a crossed polarizer/analyzer pair rotating together. The upper calibration strip has fixed birefringent retardance independent of press load, and a known slowly varying principal-axis direction from about 10 to 40 degrees counterclockwise from image-right across its left-to-right extent. Its changing brightness pattern is therefore a passive optical reference for the common polarizer angle, not a control lamp or answer display. The three loaded specimens additionally have shape-dependent stress concentration, principal-axis patterns and stress-relaxation histories. A material color is not a fixed operation code. In a plane polariscope, transmitted intensity depends on both the angle factor sin^2(2(theta-alpha)) and the stress-dependent retardance factor sin^2(delta/2), where theta is the local material principal-axis angle and alpha is the common optical-pair angle. Thus brightness or a particular hue alone is not a monotonic measure of load.\n\nAllowed operations. each event applies exactly ONE of IncreaseLoad, DecreaseLoad, RotateClockwise, or RotateCounterclockwise. The first two increase or decrease the compressive load by moving the common press linkage. The last two turn the external right-wall optical-pair control wheel clockwise or counterclockwise as viewed from outside on the apparatus +x side, looking inward along -x. This is a side-view wheel convention, not clockwise motion of a specimen in the supplied camera image. The known linkage maps RotateClockwise to increasing alpha and RotateCounterclockwise to decreasing alpha; positive alpha is counterclockwise from image-right in the front-facing specimen optical coordinates. The initial alpha is 12 degrees. Each rotation changes it by approximately 38 degrees, including the short drive ramp, without wrapping. Each load command changes the normalized load by approximately 0.29, starting from 0.32. No specimen is translated to encode a rotation, and no event directly repaints an object. \n\nPhysical response and model scope. The press is an ideal prescribed mechanical linkage, not a simulation of hydraulic-fluid flow. Load is measured from its actual crosshead displacement. This load drives a reduced-order viscoelastic response with different loading, unloading and memory time scales for the three shapes, and a shape-dependent analytical photoelastic rendering model; this is not a resolved finite-element stress solution. The dogbone responds fastest, the arch is intermediate and the perforated disk retains stress longest. Unloading need not instantly erase a colored pattern. The specimens also shorten slightly under compression while remaining seated between their pads. The upper reference strip has fixed retardance, so load-only operations do not change its optical state. Rotating the common optical pair affects both the reference strip and all three specimens, while retained specimen stress can continue relaxing. Compare the independent reference and loaded-specimen changes across frames rather than equating one object's color with a command. All states evolve continuously; neither press nor material memory is reset between events.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nThe first operation follows a 1.2 s initial hold. Each event has a 1.2 s drive and a 3.8 s observation period. The evidence image is selected from 0.5 s after that drive ends until shortly before the next drive. Frames need not represent mechanical or optical equilibrium; older stress can remain.\nCapture times (physical simulation clock; the F0 image precedes event 1): F1=2.900010 s; F2=11.099916 s; F3=15.933176 s; F4=21.133097 s; F5=22.933069 s; F6=31.132944 s.\nSupplied event start times: 1.2 s, 6.2 s, 11.2 s, 16.2 s, 21.2 s, 26.2 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nIncreaseLoad, DecreaseLoad, RotateClockwise, RotateCounterclockwise\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"IncreaseLoad\"],[\"DecreaseLoad\"],[\"RotateClockwise\"],[\"RotateCounterclockwise\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"IncreaseLoad\"], [\"DecreaseLoad\"], [\"RotateClockwise\"], [\"RotateCounterclockwise\"]].", "excerpt": "Event 1 (F0 to F1): the strip is unchanged and the specimens change only slightly. The dogbone gains some color and the disk is similar. This is a load event, most likely IncreaseLoad (load 0.32 to 0.61).", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "Reading the frames: the reference strip looks alike in F0, F1, F3 and F6 (a gray/dark band). It looks bright and white in F2, F4 and F5. The strip changes only with rotation, so I infer the rotation state from it and treat the other events as load changes.\n\nStrip states: F0 is dark (alpha 12), F1 is dark, F2 is bright, F3 is dark, F4 is bright, F5 is bright, F6 is dark.\n\nThe strip's intensity depends on sin²(2(θ−α)). Its axis runs from 10 to 40 degrees, so at α=12 it is near extinction on the left. At α=50 or 88 it would look different. A rotation of 38 degrees shifts 2α by 76 degrees. Dark-versus-bright is therefore not a simple function of α, so I use the sequence of changes.\n\nEvent 1 (F0 to F1): the strip is unchanged and the specimens change only slightly. The dogbone gains some color and the disk is similar. This is a load event, most likely IncreaseLoad (load 0.32 to 0.61).\n\nEvent 2 (F1 to F2): the strip changes from dark to bright, so this is a rotation. The specimen patterns change a lot.\n\nEvent 3 (F2 to F3): the strip returns to dark, so this is a rotation. F3 looks much like F1, which suggests the alpha is back at 12 and the event is the reverse rotation. If event 2 was clockwise (alpha 50), event 3 is counterclockwise (alpha 12). F3 resembling F1 is consistent with this.\n\nEvent 4 (F3 to F4): the strip turns bright again and F4 looks identical to F2, so alpha is back at 50. This is RotateClockwise.\n\nEvent 5 (F4 to F5): the strip stays bright, so this is a load event. The specimens look slightly less colored in the dogbone, with a lower retardance. This suggests DecreaseLoad (load 0.61 to 0.32).\n\nEvent 6 (F5 to F6): the strip turns dark, so this is a rotation back to alpha 12, which is RotateCounterclockwise.\n\nFor event 2, going from 12 to 50 requires clockwise, since the alternative of 12 to −26 would also give a bright strip. I choose clockwise. Events 3 and 4 follow from that choice. Event 6 returns to alpha 12 from 50 by counterclockwise rotation.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["IncreaseLoad"], "predicted": ["IncreaseLoad"], "correct": true}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["RotateCounterclockwise"], "predicted": ["RotateClockwise"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["RotateClockwise"], "predicted": ["RotateCounterclockwise"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["RotateCounterclockwise"], "predicted": ["RotateClockwise"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["DecreaseLoad"], "predicted": ["DecreaseLoad"], "correct": true}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["RotateCounterclockwise"], "predicted": ["RotateCounterclockwise"], "correct": true}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S18", "title": "Double reflection", "domain": "Optics", "question_id": "scene_09/sample_0172/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0172", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS\nThree fixed sources produce red, green and blue rays. Each ray travels from its source to Mirror1, then Mirror2, then one flat receiver screen. Mirror1 is left of and upstream of Mirror2; the screen is farther downstream. Both plane mirrors are 1.90 m wide and 1.40 m high and rotate about fixed centres without translating. The interior camera shows only the receiver screen and three spots, not the sources or mirrors. The screen grid spacing is 0.50 m. Colours distinguish rays, not operations.\n\nUse the apparatus frame: +x is image-right, +y image-up, and +z downstream toward the screen. The screen centre is (0, 1.55, 4.532677) m; the camera faces approximately along +z with a small fixed calibration offset. Mirror1 is centred at (-1.20, 1.55, 0) m; Mirror2 at (1.00, 1.55, 1.80) m. Both stages have zero yaw and pitch at F0. Their oriented unit normals at zero are:\nMirror1: (-0.903670, 0.000000, 0.428230)\nMirror2: (0.964532, 0.000000, -0.263965)\nThe sources have these fixed positions in metres and unit launch directions:\nRed: position (-1.200000, 1.550000, -3.000000); direction (-0.000000, 0.000000, 1.000000)\nGreen: position (-1.727366, 0.794434, -3.320348); direction (0.116074, 0.156897, 0.980770)\nBlue: position (-0.455998, 2.598389, -2.467658); direction (-0.188286, -0.254505, 0.948565)\nThese rounded values specify the fixed apparatus, including geometry outside the camera view, not any event measurement.\n\nCONTROLS\nAn event applies one action to one mirror, or one action on each mirror simultaneously. Mirror1's yaw and pitch increments are both 1.00 degree. Mirror2's yaw increment is 2.00 degrees and its pitch increment is 1.50 degrees. A selected stage completes its increment in 1.20 seconds; simultaneously selected stages start together. All settings accumulate without resets.\n\nMirror1Right and Mirror2Right increase the selected mirror's yaw about +y, the rotation taking +z toward +x; Mirror1Left and Mirror2Left decrease it. Mirror1Up and Mirror2Up increase pitch, tilting the chosen oriented normal toward +y from its horizontal zero-pitch heading; Mirror1Down and Mirror2Down decrease pitch. Unselected axes retain their settings.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event image is a native interior frame 3.50 s after its command begins. Event commands are 5.50 s apart. Mirror settings accumulate, and a single event may change both mirrors.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=1.966667 s; F1=5.500000 s; F2=11.000000 s; F3=16.500000 s; F4=22.000000 s; F5=27.500000 s; F6=33.000000 s.\nSupplied event start times: 2 s, 7.5 s, 13 s, 18.5 s, 24 s, 29.5 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nMirror1Left, Mirror1Right, Mirror1Up, Mirror1Down, Mirror2Left, Mirror2Right, Mirror2Up, Mirror2Down\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"Mirror1Left\"],[\"Mirror1Right\"],[\"Mirror1Up\"],[\"Mirror1Down\"],[\"Mirror2Left\"],[\"Mirror2Right\"],[\"Mirror2Up\"],[\"Mirror2Down\"],[\"Mirror1Left\",\"Mirror2Left\"],[\"Mirror1Left\",\"Mirror2Right\"],[\"Mirror1Left\",\"Mirror2Up\"],[\"Mirror1Left\",\"Mirror2Down\"],[\"Mirror1Right\",\"Mirror2Left\"],[\"Mirror1Right\",\"Mirror2Right\"],[\"Mirror1Right\",\"Mirror2Up\"],[\"Mirror1Right\",\"Mirror2Down\"],[\"Mirror1Up\",\"Mirror2Left\"],[\"Mirror1Up\",\"Mirror2Right\"],[\"Mirror1Up\",\"Mirror2Up\"],[\"Mirror1Up\",\"Mirror2Down\"],[\"Mirror1Down\",\"Mirror2Left\"],[\"Mirror1Down\",\"Mirror2Right\"],[\"Mirror1Down\",\"Mirror2Up\"],[\"Mirror1Down\",\"Mirror2Down\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"Mirror1Left\"], [\"Mirror1Right\"], [\"Mirror1Up\"], [\"Mirror1Down\"], [\"Mirror2Left\"], [\"Mirror2Right\"], [\"Mirror2Up\"], [\"Mirror2Down\"], [\"Mirror1Left\", \"Mirror2Left\"], [\"Mirror1Left\", \"Mirror2Right\"], [\"Mirror1Left\", \"Mirror2Up\"], [\"Mirror1Left\", \"Mirror2Down\"], [\"Mirror1Right\", \"Mirror2Left\"], [\"Mirror1Right\", \"Mirror2Right\"], [\"Mirror1Right\", \"Mirror2Up\"], [\"Mirror1Right\", \"Mirror2Down\"], [\"Mirror1Up\", \"Mirror2Left\"], [\"Mirror1Up\", \"Mirror2Right\"], [\"Mirror1Up\", \"Mirror2Up\"], [\"Mirror1Up\", \"Mirror2Down\"], [\"Mirror1Down\", \"Mirror2Left\"], [\"Mirror1Down\", \"Mirror2Right\"], [\"Mirror1Down\", \"Mirror2Up\"], [\"Mirror1Down\", \"Mirror2Down\"]].", "excerpt": "E2 is -78 px, which is about 2 × 39. A single Mirror2Left would give -39, so -78 needs Mirror1Left (-36) plus Mirror2Left (-39), giving -75. E2 is therefore Mirror1Left with Mirror2Left.", "excerpt_event": 2, "response_text": "I estimated each event from the spot shifts between frames. I did not run a full ray-trace, so the fine distinctions below rest on rough sensitivity estimates.\n\n**Scale and spot positions.** The grid spacing of 0.5 m is about 82 px, so 1 px is about 0.0061 m. Spot positions (px) are:\n\n| Frame | Red | Green | Blue |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F0 | (479, 270) | (571, 161) | (313, 475) |\n| F1 | (443, 255) | (542, 149) | (258, 461) |\n| F2 | (365, 254) | (472, 145) | (155, 477) |\n| F3 | (404, 255) | (507, 147) | (205, 470) |\n| F4 | (365, 254) | (472, 145) | (155, 477) |\n| F5 | (368, 220) | (484, 120) | (140, 424) |\n| F6 | (407, 224) | (519, 125) | (191, 421) |\n\n**Shifts per event (red).**\n- E1 (F0→F1): (-36, -15)\n- E2 (F1→F2): (-78, -1)\n- E3 (F2→F3): (+39, +1)\n- E4 (F3→F4): (-39, -1)\n- E5 (F4→F5): (+3, -34)\n- E6 (F5→F6): (+39, +4)\n\n**Sensitivities.**\n- Mirror2 yaw: a 2° rotation turns the reflected ray by 4°. Over the remaining path of about 2.7 m this gives roughly 0.19 m, or about 31 px, with a longer-path factor on top. A Mirror2 yaw step is therefore about ±39 px, and E3, E4 and E6 fit this size. Mirror2Right moves the beam toward +x, so E3 and E6 are Mirror2Right and E4 is Mirror2Left.\n- Mirror1 yaw: a 1° rotation turns the beam by 2°, and the path of about 6.5 m gives roughly 0.2 m, or about 35 px. For red, Mirror1 yaw shifts the spot in x by an amount of that order. E1 is (-36, -15), which looks like a Mirror1 yaw. Its normal is (-0.9, 0, 0.43), so a yaw changes the direction mostly in x. Mirror1Left gives a shift toward -x, so E1 is Mirror1Left, and the -15 px in y is probably a small cross-coupling.\n- E2 is -78 px, which is about 2 × 39. A single Mirror2Left would give -39, so -78 needs Mirror1Left (-36) plus Mirror2Left (-39), giving -75. E2 is therefore Mirror1Left with Mirror2Left.\n- E5 is a pure y shift of -34 px with x unchanged. Mirror2 pitch of 1.5° gives a 3° beam change over about 2.7 m, or about 0.14 m, which is about 23 px. Mirror1 pitch of 1° gives 2° over about 6.5 m, or about 0.23 m, which is about 38 px. The y shift of -34 px is closer to the Mirror1 value, so E5 is Mirror1Down.\n\n**Check on E1's y shift.** If E1 were Mirror1Left alone, the -15 px in y would come from cross-coupling. The E2 y shift is -1, though, and Mirror1Left appears in both E1 and E2, so the y shift should have repeated. Perhaps the E1 y shift comes from the rotated geometry. I could not resolve this, and I kept Mirror1Left for E1.", "frames": ["worked/F0.png", "worked/F1.png", "worked/F2.png", "worked/F3.png", "worked/F4.png", "worked/F5.png", "worked/F6.png"], "video": "worked/dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["Mirror1Down", "Mirror2Left"], "predicted": ["Mirror1Left"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["Mirror1Right", "Mirror2Left"], "predicted": ["Mirror1Left", "Mirror2Left"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["Mirror1Left"], "predicted": ["Mirror2Right"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["Mirror1Right"], "predicted": ["Mirror2Left"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["Mirror1Down", "Mirror2Down"], "predicted": ["Mirror1Down"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["Mirror1Left"], "predicted": ["Mirror2Right"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S19", "title": "Graded refraction", "domain": "Optics", "question_id": "scene_17/sample_0001/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0001", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS\nThree fixed collimated rays travel from a source assembly on the left toward one finite receiver panel on the right. The receiver is a fixed rectangular screen with a visible border and fixed reference marks; the three visible spots come from the same rays in every frame. A single finite transparent graded-index insert lies between source and receiver and all three rays pass through it. The insert starts near the optical centre, is supported by one rigid carriage, and retains its accumulated pose between events. The camera is near the receiver looking back toward the source: image-right is the receiver/source-axis direction and image-up is laboratory +y; the near transverse side is toward the camera and the far side is away from it. The insert has approximate half-extents 0.90 m along the beam, 0.39 m vertically and 0.54 m transversely, with a smooth superellipsoidal boundary.\n\nThe effective index inside the insert follows the calibrated finite profile n=1+0.10(1-q)^2 where q is the superellipsoidal normalized radius (and n=1 outside). The three rays are integrated through this field and then meet the same finite receiver. This calibration describes the apparatus; it does not give a direct answer mapping from one spot displacement to one label. Small shape changes between samples are physical radius/exponent variations, not extra events.\n\nALLOWED LABELS\nmedium_left: translate the insert toward the source side along the optical axis.\nmedium_right: translate it toward the receiver side along the optical axis.\nmedium_near: translate it toward the camera-side transverse direction.\nmedium_far: translate it away from the camera-side transverse direction.\nmedium_rotate: rotate the insert with positive yaw about the laboratory vertical axis.\nmedium_counterrotate: rotate it with negative yaw about that same axis.\nEach transition contains exactly one of these controls. The insert is not reset between transitions. Motion can leave residual pose or optical response from an earlier transition.\n\n\n\nCURRENT INTERIOR DISPLAY\nIn the current delivered interior images, all three spots have a similar yellow/amber appearance. Their colours do not identify separate rays. Track the three-spot geometry relative to the fixed receiver border and reference marks; do not assume red, amber and blue identities are visibly available.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event image shows the response to exactly one new insert-control event. The insert retains its accumulated pose. Track the shared three-spot geometry relative to the fixed receiver border and reference marks, including the preceding state.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=0.000000 s; F1=8.500000 s; F2=14.666667 s; F3=20.866669 s; F4=27.066668 s; F5=33.266670 s; F6=39.466667 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nmedium_left, medium_right, medium_near, medium_far, medium_rotate, medium_counterrotate\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"medium_left\"],[\"medium_right\"],[\"medium_near\"],[\"medium_far\"],[\"medium_rotate\"],[\"medium_counterrotate\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"medium_left\"], [\"medium_right\"], [\"medium_near\"], [\"medium_far\"], [\"medium_rotate\"], [\"medium_counterrotate\"]].", "excerpt": "All spots shift right by about 30 px and the spread shrinks slightly. I read this as a translation along the beam axis, so medium_right.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "I could not derive these from a quantitative model, so they are best-guess labels based on how the three spots move between frames. I read the spot positions (x, y in image pixels) off each frame.\n\n| Frame | Spot 1 | Spot 2 | Spot 3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F0 | (422, 147) | (404, 207) | (408, 360) |\n| F1 | (452, 170) | (433, 204) | (441, 355) |\n| F2 | (418, 249) | (448, 251) | (470, 307) |\n| F3 | (398, 238) | (438, 259) | (470, 307) |\n| F4 | (518, 107) | (525, 179) | (527, 405) |\n| F5 | (528, 86) | (545, 186) | (541, 405) |\n| F6 | (513, 144) | (526, 207) | (523, 361) |\n\nEach transition, with my reasoning:\n\n1. **F0→F1:** All spots shift right by about 30 px and the spread shrinks slightly. I read this as a translation along the beam axis, so medium_right.\n2. **F1→F2:** The spots converge strongly and move down toward the middle, which looks like a transverse shift. I chose medium_near.\n3. **F2→F3:** Spot 1 moves left and up, spot 2 moves left, and spot 3 stays put. This is a small change in the tilt or yaw of the insert, so I chose medium_counterrotate.\n4. **F3→F4:** The spots spread out vertically and jump right, which looks like the insert moving back to a centred position. That suggests medium_far, undoing the earlier near shift.\n5. **F4→F5:** Spots 1 and 2 shift right and spot 3 shifts slightly right, with the top spot moving up and the spread growing. I chose medium_rotate.\n6. **F5→F6:** The spread shrinks again and the spots move left and down toward the F0 pattern. I chose medium_left.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["medium_rotate"], "predicted": ["medium_right"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["medium_far"], "predicted": ["medium_near"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["medium_left"], "predicted": ["medium_counterrotate"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["medium_near"], "predicted": ["medium_far"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["medium_counterrotate"], "predicted": ["medium_rotate"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["medium_right"], "predicted": ["medium_left"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}, {"id": "S20", "title": "Double-slit fringes", "domain": "Optics", "question_id": "scene_18/sample_0007/k6", "sample_id": "sample_0007", "model": "Claude Sonnet 5.5", "prompt": "Infer exactly 6 hidden events from exactly 7 chronological interior images F0-F6. F0 is the initial evidence frame before the first event. Each subsequent image adds one new event. Use only these supplied images and the public apparatus description. Reconstruct the complete unordered set of controls at every supplied event; events are ordered in time.\n\nAPPARATUS AND PUBLIC CONTROL DEFINITIONS\nAPPARATUS AND VIEW\nOne fixed coherent monochromatic light source illuminates two parallel vertical slits made by four real opaque knife edges. The four edges share a width-adjustment mechanism and a separate centre-spacing adjustment mechanism. Both slit widths are equal. A single fixed flat receiver behind the slits shows vertical interference fringes inside a broader diffraction envelope. Source, receiver, wavelength, camera, illumination and exposure are fixed throughout one sample. The two slits are not independently illuminated or independently resized.\n\nLaboratory-up is positive y. Light propagates approximately along positive z from the source toward the receiver; slit width and centre spacing lie along x. The interior camera is at approximately (0,1.99,0.82), looking along positive z toward the receiver centred at (0,1.99,3.78) in scene units. The image is not mirrored: positive x is image-right and positive y is up. The receiver front is5.1 scene units wide and2.76 high. It is an enlarged instrument display of the optical distribution, not a literal5.1m-wide optical propagation experiment. No hidden handwheels, blades or mechanisms are visible in this receiver-only camera. Fixed frame edges and neutral fiducials are stationary references, not answer codes. A fixed source colour may differ between samples but does not change within a sample or encode an operation.\n\nMECHANICS AND DECLARED OPTICAL MODEL\nThe two independent shafts move continuously at up to260 degrees/s. Real shaft angles drive differential screws, followers and links, which determine actual knife-edge positions. The ideal mechanism is backlash-free and completes each permitted gesture in less than0.83s. It does not reset the receiver pattern between events.\n\nFor understanding the scaled apparatus, if the width shaft angle is theta_w and spacing shaft angle theta_d in degrees, the rendered real mechanical gaps are w=0.23+0.00065*theta_w and s=0.68+0.00170*theta_d scene units. The corresponding calibrated optical slit width is a=0.060+0.085*(w-0.14)/0.16 millimetres; optical centre spacing is d=0.30+0.32*(s-0.52)/0.42 millimetres. These are two explicit scale calibrations, not a claim that the large visible knife gaps have microscopic physical size. The sample begins at actual shaft angles within theta_w=-30..30 and theta_d=-22..22 degrees. Their initial values are not necessarily a preceding control endpoint.\n\nThe wavelength lambda is constant within the sample and lies in538..644nm; there is no hidden wavelength-changing control. The receiver represents an optical distance L=2.4m and horizontal optical span95mm from -47.5 to+47.5mm. Its fixed display magnification does not change between images. For optical horizontal coordinate X, sin(theta)=X/sqrt(L^2+X^2). The intensity is proportional to sinc^2(pi*a*sin(theta)/lambda)*cos^2(pi*d*sin(theta)/lambda), where sinc(u)=sin(u)/u and all lengths use matching units. A fixed0.58 brightness gamma, fixed spectral tint and fixed fine surface texture affect appearance, not the spatial scale. This is a declared Fraunhofer instrument model, not a full Maxwell propagation or photometrically calibrated hardware simulation.\n\nThe broad diffraction envelope and its minima constrain a; the fine fringe pitch constrains d. Both affect the same intensity image, including missing orders and the number of visible bright fringes. Counted bright bands or overall brightness alone can be misleading. Compare both the broad envelope and the fine periodic structure relative to fixed screen geometry, including the preceding state. A dual change can combine a wider envelope with tighter fringes, or a narrower envelope with wider fringes; neither control cancels the other's physical change.\n\nALLOWED PRIMITIVE CONTROLS\nNarrowSlits: drive only the width shaft to -123degrees, decreasing both actual slit widths; spacing stays at its previous endpoint unless a spacing control is simultaneously selected.\nWidenSlits: drive only the width shaft to+92degrees, increasing both widths; spacing otherwise persists.\nIncreaseSpacing: drive only the spacing shaft to+135degrees, moving the slit centres farther apart; width otherwise persists.\nDecreaseSpacing: drive only the spacing shaft to-76degrees, moving the slit centres closer; width otherwise persists.\n\nEach event contains either exactly ONE primitive or TWO simultaneous primitives: one from {NarrowSlits,WidenSlits} and one from {IncreaseSpacing,DecreaseSpacing}. Opposite primitives on the same axis are forbidden. An unselected axis remains at its preceding setting. Every selected axis makes a real nonzero change; selecting an already reached endpoint is not counted as an operation. Operation sets are unordered within an event, but the events are ordered. No per-label frequency or sequence is supplied.\n\nOBSERVATION PROTOCOL FOR THIS PREFIX\nEach event image shows the completed real nonzero change of its selected slit mechanism axes. The pattern is generated by the same fixed optical calibration throughout this sample; an unselected axis retains its preceding setting. Use both the diffraction envelope and fine fringe spacing relative to the receiver geometry.\nCapture times (physical/native capture time in seconds): F0=0.000000 s; F1=8.500000 s; F2=14.683334 s; F3=20.883335 s; F4=27.083334 s; F5=33.283337 s; F6=39.483334 s.\n\nALLOWED ATOMIC LABELS\nNarrowSlits, WidenSlits, IncreaseSpacing, DecreaseSpacing\n\nALLOWED EVENT LABEL SETS\n[[\"NarrowSlits\"],[\"WidenSlits\"],[\"IncreaseSpacing\"],[\"DecreaseSpacing\"],[\"IncreaseSpacing\",\"NarrowSlits\"],[\"DecreaseSpacing\",\"NarrowSlits\"],[\"IncreaseSpacing\",\"WidenSlits\"],[\"DecreaseSpacing\",\"WidenSlits\"]]\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT\nReturn only one valid JSON object with exactly one root field, \"operations\". Its value must contain exactly 6 nonempty lists, in chronological event order. Every inner list must equal one allowed event label set and comply with the control rules above. Order within an event list does not matter. Even if uncertain, select your best-supported allowed answer for every event. Never return an empty list, null, unknown, abstention, or a missing event. Do not output markdown, commentary, or additional fields.\nFINAL ANSWER CONTRACT: Give exactly 6 chronological event arrays under the sole JSON key \"operations\". Choose the most likely legal answer for EVERY event even when uncertain. Do not abstain, return an empty answer, or omit simultaneous actions. Your final visible answer must be only the JSON object. Legal action sets for one event: [[\"NarrowSlits\"], [\"WidenSlits\"], [\"IncreaseSpacing\"], [\"DecreaseSpacing\"], [\"IncreaseSpacing\", \"NarrowSlits\"], [\"DecreaseSpacing\", \"NarrowSlits\"], [\"IncreaseSpacing\", \"WidenSlits\"], [\"DecreaseSpacing\", \"WidenSlits\"]].", "excerpt": "F0→F1: the fringes became tighter, so spacing increased. The envelope in F0 had its first minimum at about ±130 px, and in F1 the minimum is wider at about ±160 px. A wider envelope means the slits widened. This gives IncreaseSpacing and WidenSlits.", "excerpt_event": 1, "response_text": "Reading the frames, F1 and F3 look identical, and F2, F4 and F6 look identical, so the states alternate between a few distinct patterns.\n\n**State estimates**\n- F0 has fringe pitch of about 26 px and an envelope first minimum near ±130 px from center.\n- F1 has finer fringes (about 20 px pitch) and a wider envelope, with the first minimum near ±160 px. Both the envelope and the fringe pitch changed from F0.\n- F2 has coarse fringes (about 37 px pitch) and a narrow central envelope.\n- F5 has fringes of about 36 px pitch and a central envelope roughly 2.5 times wider than F2's.\n\n**Event reasoning**\n1. F0→F1: the fringes became tighter, so spacing increased. The envelope in F0 had its first minimum at about ±130 px, and in F1 the minimum is wider at about ±160 px. A wider envelope means the slits widened. This gives IncreaseSpacing and WidenSlits.\n2. F1→F2: the fringes became coarser (spacing decreased) and the envelope narrowed (slits narrowed). This gives DecreaseSpacing and NarrowSlits.\n3. F2→F3: F3 equals F1, so the change reverses the previous one: IncreaseSpacing and WidenSlits.\n4. F3→F4: F4 equals F2, so the change is again DecreaseSpacing and NarrowSlits.\n5. F4→F5: the fringes are about the same pitch (spacing unchanged), but the envelope is wider, so the slits widened. This gives WidenSlits alone.\n6. F5→F6: F6 returns to the F2 pattern, with a narrower envelope and the same fringe pitch, so NarrowSlits alone.\n\n**Consistency check on endpoints**\nBecause each control drives its shaft to a fixed endpoint, the states are limited to a few shaft angles. The width endpoints are -123 and +92, and the spacing endpoints are +135 and -76.\n- F1 and F3 are width +92 with spacing +135.\n- F2, F4 and F6 are width -123 with spacing -76.\n- F5 is width +92 with spacing -76, which fits the coarse fringes with a wide envelope.\n- F0 has intermediate initial values.\n\nThe sequence is consistent with these endpoints.", "frames": ["F0.png", "F1.png", "F2.png", "F3.png", "F4.png", "F5.png", "F6.png"], "video": "dual_view.mp4", "steps": [{"event": 1, "expected": ["IncreaseSpacing", "NarrowSlits"], "predicted": ["IncreaseSpacing", "WidenSlits"], "correct": false}, {"event": 2, "expected": ["DecreaseSpacing", "WidenSlits"], "predicted": ["DecreaseSpacing", "NarrowSlits"], "correct": false}, {"event": 3, "expected": ["IncreaseSpacing", "NarrowSlits"], "predicted": ["IncreaseSpacing", "WidenSlits"], "correct": false}, {"event": 4, "expected": ["DecreaseSpacing", "WidenSlits"], "predicted": ["DecreaseSpacing", "NarrowSlits"], "correct": false}, {"event": 5, "expected": ["NarrowSlits"], "predicted": ["WidenSlits"], "correct": false}, {"event": 6, "expected": ["WidenSlits"], "predicted": ["NarrowSlits"], "correct": false}], "exact_match": false}];
window.CAVE_RELEASE = {"authors": ["Guanxin Lin*", "Chen Yang*", "Shenxiang Zeng", "Honghui Zhu", "Xin Li", "Wenbo Deng", "Jiansheng Fan", "Chen Wang†"], "affiliations": ["Tsinghua University"], "authorNote": "* Equal contribution   ·   † Corresponding author", "github": "https://github.com/PIER-Lab/CaveBench", "weights": "https://github.com/PIER-Lab/CaveBench/releases/tag/cavebench-qwen35-adapters-v1", "paper": "papers/CaveBench.pdf", "supplement": "papers/Supplement.pdf", "dataset": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/linguanxin/CaveBench"};
